window.MM['rs_grand_mock_1'] = [
  {q:"The left testicular vein drains into the:",a:"Left renal vein",b:"Inferior vena cava directly",c:"Left internal iliac vein",d:"Left common iliac vein",ans:"A",e:"The left testicular vein drains into the left renal vein at a right angle, while the right testicular vein drains directly into the IVC - explaining the higher incidence of left-sided varicoceles.<br><br>Take Notes: Left testicular vein → left renal vein (right angle = turbulent flow = varicocele) | Right testicular vein → IVC directly (acute angle = less turbulence)",t:180},
  {q:"The transpyloric plane of Addison lies at which vertebral level?",a:"L1",b:"T12",c:"L2",d:"L3",ans:"A",e:"The transpyloric plane at L1 passes through the pylorus, fundus of gallbladder, hilum of kidneys, origin of portal vein, neck of pancreas, and origin of superior mesenteric artery.<br><br>Take Notes: Transpyloric plane L1 mnemonic → I (8) EAT (10) EGGS AT (12) NOON | L4 → aortic bifurcation, highest point of iliac crest",t:180},
  {q:"The portal vein is formed behind the neck of the pancreas by the union of:",a:"Superior mesenteric vein and splenic vein",b:"Superior mesenteric vein and inferior mesenteric vein",c:"Splenic vein and inferior mesenteric vein",d:"Left and right portal veins",ans:"A",e:"The portal vein forms at L1 behind the neck of the pancreas by union of the superior mesenteric vein and splenic vein; it passes in the hepatoduodenal ligament to the porta hepatis.<br><br>Take Notes: IMV → usually drains into splenic vein | Portal triad in free edge of lesser omentum → portal vein (posterior), hepatic artery (left), CBD (right)",t:180},
  {q:"The femoral canal contains:",a:"Femoral nerve",b:"Femoral artery",c:"Femoral vein",d:"Lymph node of Cloquet and fat",ans:"D",e:"The femoral canal is the most medial compartment of the femoral sheath; it contains fat, lymphatics, and the lymph node of Cloquet - it provides potential space for femoral hernias to protrude.<br><br>Take Notes: NAVEL → Nerve (outside sheath), Artery, Vein, Empty space (femoral canal), Lymphatics | Femoral hernia → below and lateral to pubic tubercle (inguinal = above and medial)",t:180},
  {q:"The obturator nerve (L2-L4) is most commonly injured during which surgical procedure?",a:"Radical hysterectomy or pelvic lymph node dissection",b:"Total hip replacement",c:"Appendicectomy",d:"Right hemicolectomy",ans:"A",e:"The obturator nerve runs along the lateral pelvic wall through the obturator fossa and is most vulnerable during radical hysterectomy and pelvic lymph node dissection, causing hip adduction weakness and medial thigh sensory loss.<br><br>Take Notes: Obturator nerve → L2-L4, exits through obturator foramen | Injury → adductor weakness + medial thigh sensory loss | Obturator hernia → Howship-Romberg sign (medial thigh pain on hip internal rotation)",t:180},
  {q:"Which structure lies in the free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament)?",a:"Hepatic artery, portal vein, and common bile duct",b:"Left gastric artery",c:"Splenic artery and vein",d:"Coeliac axis",ans:"A",e:"The hepatoduodenal ligament (free edge of lesser omentum) contains the portal triad: portal vein (posteriorly), hepatic artery proper (left anteriorly), and common bile duct (right anteriorly) - compressed in the Pringle manoeuvre.<br><br>Take Notes: Pringle manoeuvre → compresses hepatoduodenal ligament to control hepatic inflow | CBD → right and anterior | Portal vein → posterior and largest | Hepatic artery → left and anterior",t:180},
  {q:"The thoracic duct drains into the:",a:"Right subclavian and right internal jugular vein junction",b:"Superior vena cava",c:"Left brachiocephalic vein directly",d:"Left subclavian and left internal jugular vein junction",ans:"D",e:"The thoracic duct drains into the left venous angle - the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins, draining all lymph except right upper quadrant which drains via the right lymphatic duct.<br><br>Take Notes: Right lymphatic duct → drains right arm, right chest, right head | Thoracic duct injury → at left neck or during oesophagectomy → chylothorax (milky, high triglycerides)",t:180},
  {q:"Which nerve is most at risk during thyroidectomy near the ligament of Berry?",a:"External laryngeal nerve",b:"Internal laryngeal nerve",c:"Left recurrent laryngeal nerve",d:"Right recurrent laryngeal nerve",ans:"D",e:"The right RLN loops around the right subclavian artery and ascends obliquely in the tracheo-oesophageal groove, passing close to the ligament of Berry (posterior capsule of thyroid) - its variable course makes it more vulnerable than the left.<br><br>Take Notes: RLN supplies all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid (external laryngeal nerve) | Bilateral RLN injury → stridor, may need tracheostomy | Non-recurrent RLN → 0.5-1% on right side with arteria lusoria",t:180},
  {q:"The calot triangle is bounded by:",a:"Common hepatic duct medially, cystic duct inferiorly, inferior surface of liver superiorly",b:"Common bile duct medially, hepatic artery inferiorly, liver superiorly",c:"Portal vein medially, cystic duct inferiorly, liver superiorly",d:"Common hepatic duct medially, duodenum inferiorly, liver superiorly",ans:"A",e:"Calot's triangle is bounded by the common hepatic duct (medially), cystic duct (inferolaterally), and inferior surface of liver (superiorly) - the cystic artery is the main content and must be identified in the critical view of safety.<br><br>Take Notes: Critical view of safety (Strasberg) → lower third of cystic plate exposed + two structures only entering gallbladder neck before clipping | Most common bile duct injury → mistaking CBD for cystic duct",t:180},
  {q:"The saphenofemoral junction is located:",a:"4 cm below and lateral to the pubic tubercle",b:"4 cm above and medial to the pubic tubercle",c:"At the mid-inguinal point",d:"Behind the medial malleolus",ans:"A",e:"The saphenofemoral junction lies 4 cm below and lateral to the pubic tubercle, where the great saphenous vein joins the femoral vein - the key surface marking for high ligation in varicose vein surgery.<br><br>Take Notes: Great saphenous → medial to femoral vein, most consistent vein for CABG conduit | Small saphenous → joins popliteal vein behind knee | Trendelenburg test → assesses SFJ competence",t:180},
  {q:"The diaphragm openings from superior to inferior: the IVC, oesophagus, and aorta pass through at vertebral levels:",a:"T8, T10, T12",b:"T10, T8, T12",c:"T8, T12, T10",d:"T12, T10, T8",ans:"A",e:"IVC passes at T8 (with right phrenic nerve), oesophagus at T10 (with vagal trunks and left gastric vessels), aorta at T12 (with thoracic duct and azygos vein).<br><br>Take Notes: Mnemonic I 8 (ate) TEN (10) EGGS AT (12) NOON → IVC T8, Oesophagus T10, Aorta T12 | Para-oesophageal hernia → through oesophageal hiatus | Bochdalek hernia → left posterolateral defect",t:180},
  {q:"The femoral triangle boundaries are:",a:"Inguinal ligament superiorly, sartorius laterally, adductor longus medially",b:"Inguinal ligament superiorly, rectus femoris laterally, gracilis medially",c:"Inguinal ligament superiorly, iliotibial band laterally, adductor magnus medially",d:"Poupart's ligament superiorly, tensor fascia lata laterally, pectineus medially",ans:"A",e:"The femoral triangle is bounded by the inguinal ligament superiorly, sartorius laterally, and adductor longus medially - contents from lateral to medial are nerve, artery, vein, empty space, lymphatics (NAVEL).<br><br>Take Notes: Roof → fascia lata | Floor → iliopsoas (lateral) + pectineus (medial) | Femoral nerve → outside femoral sheath, lateral to artery | Femoral canal → most medial, site of femoral hernia",t:180},
  {q:"Couinaud's classification divides the liver into segments based on:",a:"Portal vein and hepatic vein distribution into 8 functionally independent segments",b:"Hepatic artery branches only",c:"Bile duct drainage territories",d:"Histological zones of the hepatic acinus",ans:"A",e:"Couinaud divides the liver into 8 segments, each with its own portal pedicle (portal vein, hepatic artery, bile duct) and drained by a hepatic vein, allowing anatomical resections preserving viable remnant.<br><br>Take Notes: Segment I → caudate lobe (drains directly to IVC, independent of hepatic veins) | Right lobe → segments V-VIII | Left lobe → segments II-IV | Middle hepatic vein + Cantlie line → divides right and left lobes",t:180},
  {q:"The McBurney point overlies which structure?",a:"Terminal ileum",b:"Ileocaecal valve",c:"Most distensible point of caecum",d:"Base of the appendix at tenia coli convergence",ans:"D",e:"McBurney's point is 1/3 of the distance from the anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus, overlying the base of the appendix where all three taeniae coli converge on the caecum.<br><br>Take Notes: Rovsing sign → LIF pressure causes RIF pain (peritoneal irritation) | Psoas sign → retrocaecal appendix (pain on hip extension) | Obturator sign → pelvic appendix (pain on internal rotation of flexed hip)",t:180},
  {q:"The ureter in a female pelvis is most vulnerable during hysterectomy because it passes:",a:"Posterior to the ovarian vessels",b:"Superior to the uterine artery",c:"Alongside the round ligament",d:"Under the uterine artery (water under the bridge) 1.5 cm lateral to the cervix",ans:"D",e:"The ureter passes under the uterine artery approximately 1.5 cm lateral to the cervix within the broad ligament - the classic 'water under the bridge' mnemonic - and is most at risk during clamping of the uterine pedicle.<br><br>Take Notes: Also at risk → during ligation of infundibulopelvic ligament (ovarian vessels cross ureter anteriorly) | Prevention → ureteric stent pre-operatively in high-risk cases | Intraoperative injury → methylene blue test or IVU",t:180},
  {q:"The popliteal fossa contents from superficial to deep are:",a:"Tibial nerve, popliteal vein, popliteal artery",b:"Artery, vein, tibial nerve",c:"Tibial nerve, popliteal artery, popliteal vein",d:"Vein, artery, tibial nerve",ans:"A",e:"In the popliteal fossa from superficial to deep: tibial nerve (most superficial), popliteal vein, popliteal artery (deepest, directly against posterior capsule of knee).<br><br>Take Notes: Mnemonic NVA superficial to deep → Nerve, Vein, Artery | Common peroneal → runs along biceps femoris tendon to fibular neck | Baker cyst → posterior knee swelling, communicates with joint",t:180},
  {q:"Which structure forms the posterior wall of the inguinal canal?",a:"External oblique aponeurosis",b:"Conjoint tendon (transversus abdominis and internal oblique)",c:"Posterior rectus sheath",d:"Transversalis fascia (reinforced medially by conjoint tendon)",ans:"D",e:"The posterior wall of the inguinal canal is formed by transversalis fascia throughout, reinforced medially by the conjoint tendon (fused lower fibres of internal oblique and transversus abdominis).<br><br>Take Notes: Anterior wall → external oblique aponeurosis (+ internal oblique laterally) | Roof → arching fibres of internal oblique + transversus abdominis | Floor → inguinal ligament + lacunar ligament medially",t:180},
  {q:"The cavernous sinus CN VI runs within the substance of the sinus (not in its lateral wall) - which structure accompanies it?",a:"Internal carotid artery",b:"Ophthalmic division of trigeminal (V1)",c:"Oculomotor nerve",d:"Trochlear nerve",ans:"A",e:"CN VI and the internal carotid artery pass through the body of the cavernous sinus; CN III, IV, V1, and V2 run in the lateral wall - making CN VI the most vulnerable to compression by ICA aneurysm.<br><br>Take Notes: Lateral wall top to bottom → CN III, IV, V1, V2 | CN VI palsy → earliest sign of cavernous sinus thrombosis | Cavernous sinus thrombosis → danger area of face (nasofacial vein drains to cavernous sinus)",t:180},
  {q:"The circle of Willis - which component is most commonly absent or hypoplastic?",a:"Anterior communicating artery",b:"Basilar artery",c:"Anterior cerebral artery",d:"Posterior communicating artery",ans:"D",e:"The posterior communicating artery is the most commonly absent or hypoplastic component of the circle of Willis (~30% of individuals), limiting anterior-posterior collateral flow.<br><br>Take Notes: PComA aneurysm → CN III palsy (pupil-involving, painful) | AComA → most common intracranial aneurysm site | Complete circle of Willis → only 25% of people | Incomplete circle → increased stroke risk with ICA occlusion",t:180},
  {q:"The long thoracic nerve (C5-C7) supplies serratus anterior. Its injury during axillary node clearance causes:",a:"Wrist drop",b:"Weakness of shoulder abduction beyond 90 degrees only",c:"Loss of elbow flexion",d:"Winging of the scapula on pushing against a wall",ans:"D",e:"Serratus anterior holds the medial scapular border against the thorax; its denervation causes winging when pushing forward, and limits shoulder abduction above 90 degrees even with intact deltoid.<br><br>Take Notes: Long thoracic nerve (nerve of Bell) → C5-C7, axillary dissection, mastectomy, carrying heavy bags | Winged scapula → medial border prominent on pushing | Axillary nerve → deltoid + teres minor, regimental badge area sensory loss",t:180},
  {q:"The normal GFR in a healthy adult is approximately:",a:"60 mL/min",b:"90 mL/min",c:"180 mL/min",d:"125 mL/min",ans:"D",e:"Normal GFR is ~125 mL/min (180 L/day); 99% is reabsorbed, producing only 1-2 L urine per day. CKD staging is based on GFR reduction.<br><br>Take Notes: Measured by inulin clearance (gold standard) | Estimated clinically by creatinine clearance (Cockcroft-Gault) or CKD-EPI | GFR <60 for >3 months = CKD Stage 3",t:180},
  {q:"Heparin requires which co-factor to exert its anticoagulant effect?",a:"Antithrombin III",b:"Protein C",c:"Protein S",d:"Factor V",ans:"A",e:"Heparin binds and activates antithrombin III (AT-III), dramatically accelerating its inhibitory activity against thrombin (factor IIa) and factor Xa - the primary anticoagulant mechanism.<br><br>Take Notes: AT-III deficiency → heparin resistance | UFH → inhibits IIa + Xa | LMWH → primarily inhibits Xa | Fondaparinux → pure Xa inhibitor | Protamine → reverses UFH (partial reversal of LMWH)",t:180},
  {q:"The oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve shifts to the right in response to:",a:"Decreased temperature",b:"Decreased PCO2",c:"Increased pH (alkalosis)",d:"Increased 2,3-DPG",ans:"D",e:"Increased 2,3-DPG (in chronic hypoxia, anaemia, high altitude) stabilises the T-state (deoxy) haemoglobin, reducing O2 affinity and shifting the curve rightward to enhance O2 delivery to tissues.<br><br>Take Notes: Right shift (decreased affinity) → CADET (CO2, Acid, DPG, Exercise, Temperature) | Left shift → fetal Hb, CO poisoning, alkalosis, hypothermia | P50 → PO2 at 50% saturation; right shift raises P50",t:180},
  {q:"The Frank-Starling law of the heart states that:",a:"Heart rate determines cardiac output",b:"Stroke volume is independent of end-diastolic volume",c:"Cardiac output is fixed regardless of venous return",d:"Stroke volume increases as end-diastolic volume increases (within physiological range)",ans:"D",e:"The Frank-Starling mechanism describes the intrinsic ability of the heart to increase stroke volume in response to increased venous return (preload) - longer initial sarcomere length improves actin-myosin overlap and contractile force.<br><br>Take Notes: Starling curve shifts UP with positive inotropes (adrenaline, digoxin) | Shifts DOWN with myocardial failure | Clinical application → IV fluids increase preload → increase cardiac output in hypovolaemia",t:180},
  {q:"Aldosterone acts primarily on which nephron segment?",a:"Proximal convoluted tubule",b:"Thick ascending limb",c:"Loop of Henle",d:"Distal convoluted tubule and collecting duct",ans:"D",e:"Aldosterone binds cytosolic mineralocorticoid receptors in the principal cells of the DCT and collecting duct, upregulating ENaC (sodium channels) and Na/K-ATPase to increase sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion.<br><br>Take Notes: Aldosterone → Na+ retention + K+ excretion + H2O retention | Stimulated by → angiotensin II, hyperkalaemia, ACTH | Spironolactone → blocks aldosterone receptor | Conn syndrome → primary hyperaldosteronism",t:180},
  {q:"The Hering-Breuer reflex involves stretch receptors in the lung that:",a:"Inhibit further inspiration when lungs are sufficiently inflated",b:"Stimulate inspiration when lungs are deflated",c:"Stimulate expiration only at very high lung volumes",d:"Are inactive during normal quiet breathing",ans:"A",e:"The Hering-Breuer inflation reflex uses slowly adapting stretch receptors in bronchial smooth muscle that signal via vagal afferents to inhibit the inspiratory centre when lungs are adequately inflated, preventing overinflation.<br><br>Take Notes: Active mainly in neonates and during anaesthesia (less relevant in awake adults) | Deflation reflex → stimulates inspiration when lungs collapse | Clinical → explains why high tidal volumes increase respiratory rate less effectively",t:180},
  {q:"Phase 2 (plateau) of the ventricular action potential is maintained by:",a:"Rapid Na+ influx",b:"Transient K+ outward current",c:"Closure of all ion channels",d:"Balanced Ca2+ influx via L-type channels and K+ efflux",ans:"D",e:"The plateau (phase 2) reflects a balance between inward calcium current through L-type (slow) voltage-gated calcium channels and outward potassium current - this prolonged depolarisation prevents tetanic contraction and gives time for complete systole.<br><br>Take Notes: Phase 0 → rapid Na+ in | Phase 1 → transient K+ out | Phase 2 (plateau) → Ca2+ in = K+ out | Phase 3 → K+ out (IKr, IKs) | Phase 4 → resting (−90mV in ventricle) | L-type Ca2+ → target of calcium channel blockers",t:180},
  {q:"Bicarbonate is the most important buffer in blood because:",a:"Its components (HCO3- and CO2) are independently regulated by kidney and lung respectively",b:"It is the most concentrated buffer",c:"It does not follow the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation",d:"It only works in the intracellular compartment",ans:"A",e:"The bicarbonate buffer system (H2CO3/HCO3-) is the dominant blood buffer because CO2 is continuously regulated by ventilation and HCO3- by the kidneys - allowing independent, rapid titration of both components to correct pH.<br><br>Take Notes: CO2 regulation → lungs (minutes) | HCO3- regulation → kidneys (days) | Henderson-Hasselbalch: pH = 6.1 + log [HCO3-]/0.03 x PaCO2 | Normal ratio 20:1 HCO3-:H2CO3 = pH 7.4",t:180},
  {q:"Gastric acid secretion is stimulated by all of the following EXCEPT:",a:"Acetylcholine (vagal)",b:"Histamine (H2 receptor)",c:"Gastrin (CCK-B receptor)",d:"Secretin",ans:"D",e:"Secretin (released by S cells in response to luminal acid) stimulates pancreatic bicarbonate secretion and actually inhibits gastric acid secretion - it is the physiological 'antacid' of the duodenum.<br><br>Take Notes: Stimulants of parietal cells → ACh + histamine + gastrin (all three act synergistically) | H2 blockers → suppress basal and nocturnal acid | PPIs → block final H+/K+-ATPase step (most potent)",t:180},
  {q:"The normal arterial blood gas values include PaO2:",a:"75-100 mmHg",b:"35-45 mmHg",c:"45-55 mmHg",d:"100-120 mmHg",ans:"A",e:"Normal arterial PaO2 is 75-100 mmHg (10-13.3 kPa); values below 60 mmHg (8 kPa) indicate hypoxaemia requiring intervention. PaCO2 is normally 35-45 mmHg.<br><br>Take Notes: Type 1 respiratory failure → PaO2 <60 mmHg, normal or low PaCO2 (V/Q mismatch) | Type 2 → PaO2 <60 + PaCO2 >45 mmHg (hypoventilation) | A-a gradient → assesses efficiency of gas exchange",t:180},
  {q:"Systemic vascular resistance (SVR) is determined primarily by:",a:"Cardiac output",b:"Venous capacitance",c:"Aortic compliance",d:"Arteriolar diameter (small resistance vessels)",ans:"D",e:"SVR is determined predominantly by arteriolar tone - the small resistance vessels. SVR = (MAP - CVP) / CO × 80; arteriolar constriction increases SVR and blood pressure, dilation reduces both.<br><br>Take Notes: BP = CO × SVR | Septic shock → low SVR (vasodilated) + high CO (compensatory) | Cardiogenic shock → low CO + high SVR (compensatory vasoconstriction) | Noradrenaline → increases SVR (alpha-1 agonist)",t:180},
  {q:"Intrinsic factor is secreted by which gastric cells?",a:"Chief cells",b:"G cells",c:"Mucous neck cells",d:"Parietal (oxyntic) cells",ans:"D",e:"Parietal (oxyntic) cells in the gastric body and fundus secrete both HCl and intrinsic factor; IF binds vitamin B12 in the stomach and the IF-B12 complex is absorbed by cubilin receptors in the terminal ileum.<br><br>Take Notes: Parietal cells → HCl + IF | Chief cells → pepsinogen | G cells (antrum) → gastrin | ECL cells → histamine | Pernicious anaemia → autoimmune parietal cell destruction → B12 deficiency",t:180},
  {q:"The carotid body chemoreceptors are most sensitive to:",a:"Hypoxia (peripheral chemoreceptors)",b:"Hypercapnia (central chemoreceptors)",c:"Decreased pH (central chemoreceptors)",d:"Increased temperature",ans:"A",e:"Peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid body are most sensitive to hypoxia (PaO2 <60 mmHg) and also respond to hypercapnia and acidosis; central chemoreceptors in the medulla are primarily sensitive to PaCO2/pH (via CSF pH).<br><br>Take Notes: Peripheral → carotid body (CN IX) + aortic body (CN X), respond to PaO2, PaCO2, pH | Central → medullary surface, primarily respond to PaCO2 (via CSF H+) | COPD with chronic hypercapnia → hypoxic drive (peripheral) becomes dominant",t:180},
  {q:"Which clotting factor is NOT vitamin K-dependent?",a:"Factor V",b:"Factor II (prothrombin)",c:"Factor VII",d:"Factor IX",ans:"A",e:"Vitamin K-dependent factors are II, VII, IX, X, Protein C, and Protein S - all require vitamin K for gamma-carboxylation of glutamic acid residues. Factor V is not vitamin K-dependent.<br><br>Take Notes: Mnemonic → 1972 (Factors II, VII, IX, X) + Proteins C and S | Warfarin → inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase | Protein C shortest half-life → warfarin initially prothrombotic in protein C deficiency | Factor V Leiden → most common inherited thrombophilia",t:180},
  {q:"Surfactant (DPPC) is produced by which cells?",a:"Type II pneumocytes",b:"Type I pneumocytes",c:"Clara cells",d:"Alveolar macrophages",ans:"A",e:"Type II pneumocytes (granular pneumocytes) produce surfactant (dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine/DPPC), which reduces surface tension and prevents alveolar collapse according to Laplace's law.<br><br>Take Notes: Surfactant deficiency → neonatal RDS (premature infants, type II cells insufficient) | Mature at 35 weeks gestation | L/S ratio >2:1 in amniotic fluid → fetal lung maturity | Betamethasone → accelerates fetal lung maturity | Type I → gas exchange (95% surface area)",t:180},
  {q:"Renin is secreted by which cells of the kidney?",a:"Juxtaglomerular (granular) cells of the afferent arteriole",b:"Macula densa cells",c:"Mesangial cells",d:"Podocytes",ans:"A",e:"Juxtaglomerular cells in the wall of the afferent arteriole secrete renin in response to decreased renal perfusion pressure, reduced NaCl delivery (sensed by macula densa), and sympathetic stimulation.<br><br>Take Notes: Renin → cleaves angiotensinogen → angiotensin I → (ACE) → angiotensin II | RAAS → angiotensin II → aldosterone → Na+ retention + vasoconstriction | ACE inhibitors → most used RAAS point of intervention",t:180},
  {q:"The most important determinant of cerebral blood flow is:",a:"Mean arterial pressure",b:"Arterial oxygen content",c:"Intracranial pressure alone",d:"PaCO2 (cerebral vasodilator)",ans:"D",e:"CO2 is the most potent regulator of cerebral blood flow - hypercapnia causes cerebral vasodilation increasing CBF, while hypocapnia causes vasoconstriction reducing CBF. This is used therapeutically in raised ICP.<br><br>Take Notes: CBF autoregulation → maintained over MAP 50-150 mmHg | Below 50 mmHg → CBF falls (ischaemia) | CPP = MAP - ICP (target >60 mmHg) | Hyperventilation → reduces PaCO2 → cerebral vasoconstriction → reduces ICP (temporary measure)",t:180},
  {q:"Erythropoietin (EPO) is produced primarily by:",a:"Bone marrow",b:"Liver (fetal)",c:"Spleen",d:"Peritubular interstitial cells of the kidney cortex",ans:"D",e:"EPO is produced by peritubular interstitial cells (likely fibroblasts) in the renal cortex in response to hypoxia, stimulating red cell precursor proliferation and differentiation in the bone marrow.<br><br>Take Notes: CKD → reduced EPO → normocytic, normochromic anaemia | Treatment → recombinant EPO (darbepoetin alfa) + IV iron | Polycythaemia vera → EPO-independent erythrocytosis (JAK2 mutation) | Ectopic EPO → RCC, hepatoma, cerebellar haemangioblastoma",t:180},
  {q:"The hepatic acinus zone most susceptible to ischaemic damage is:",a:"Zone 1 (periportal)",b:"Zone 2 (midzonal)",c:"All zones equally",d:"Zone 3 (centrilobular/perivenous)",ans:"D",e:"Zone 3 (centrilobular), furthest from the portal blood supply, receives the most deoxygenated blood and is most susceptible to ischaemia (right heart failure, shock) and is where centrilobular necrosis occurs.<br><br>Take Notes: Zone 1 → best oxygenated, affected first in toxin damage (paracetamol → Zone 3, phosphorus → Zone 1) | Zone 3 → most susceptible to ischaemia and CCl4 toxicity | Paracetamol → centrilobular (Zone 3) necrosis via toxic NAPQI metabolite",t:180},
  {q:"In metabolic alkalosis, the expected compensatory PaCO2 is:",a:"0.7 × HCO3- + 21",b:"1.5 × HCO3- + 8",c:"HCO3- - 15",d:"PaCO2 remains unchanged",ans:"A",e:"In metabolic alkalosis, respiratory compensation is achieved by hypoventilation - the expected PaCO2 = 0.7 × HCO3- + 21 (±2 mmHg). Compensation is limited by hypoxia which eventually drives ventilation.<br><br>Take Notes: Metabolic acidosis compensation → Winter formula: PaCO2 = 1.5 × HCO3- + 8 ± 2 | Respiratory acidosis (chronic) → HCO3- rises 3.5 per 10 mmHg CO2 | Metabolic alkalosis causes → vomiting, diuretics, hyperaldosteronism",t:180},
  {q:"The rate-limiting enzyme of the urea cycle is:",a:"Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I (CPS-I)",b:"Arginase",c:"Ornithine transcarbamylase",d:"Argininosuccinate synthetase",ans:"A",e:"CPS-I in the mitochondrial matrix commits NH3 to urea synthesis by forming carbamoyl phosphate; it requires N-acetylglutamate as an allosteric activator (activated when amino acid catabolism increases).<br><br>Take Notes: CPS-I deficiency → hyperammonaemia | N-acetylglutamate activates CPS-I (activated by arginine) | Urea cycle → entirely hepatic | OTC deficiency → most common urea cycle disorder, X-linked",t:180},
  {q:"Which enzyme is deficient in phenylketonuria (PKU)?",a:"Phenylalanine hydroxylase",b:"Tyrosinase",c:"Homogentisate oxidase",d:"Fumarylacetoacetase",ans:"A",e:"PKU results from deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH) which converts phenylalanine to tyrosine; phenylalanine accumulates and is converted to phenylpyruvate, causing intellectual disability.<br><br>Take Notes: Treatment → phenylalanine-restricted diet + tyrosine supplementation | Maternal PKU → risk to fetus even if fetus is heterozygous | Guthrie test → neonatal screening | BH4 (tetrahydrobiopterin) → cofactor for PAH; some patients respond to BH4 supplementation",t:180},
  {q:"The Krebs cycle (TCA) generates how many ATP equivalents per acetyl-CoA molecule (via electron transport)?",a:"10 ATP",b:"8 ATP",c:"12 ATP",d:"15 ATP",ans:"A",e:"Per acetyl-CoA: 3 NADH (×2.5 ATP each = 7.5) + 1 FADH2 (×1.5 ATP = 1.5) + 1 GTP (= 1) = ~10 ATP equivalents (modern P/O ratios).<br><br>Take Notes: Total aerobic glucose oxidation → ~30-32 ATP | Glycolysis → 2 ATP net | PDH → 2 NADH | TCA → 6 NADH + 2 FADH2 + 2 GTP per glucose | Old textbook value → 38 ATP (using P/O ratios of 3 and 2)",t:180},
  {q:"Pyruvate carboxylase converts pyruvate to oxaloacetate and requires which cofactor?",a:"Thiamine (B1)",b:"Biotin",c:"Pyridoxal phosphate (B6)",d:"FAD",ans:"B",e:"Pyruvate carboxylase uses biotin as a prosthetic group to carboxylate pyruvate to OAA - a critical anaplerotic reaction replenishing TCA cycle intermediates and essential for gluconeogenesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Biotin-dependent enzymes → Pyruvate carboxylase, ACC (fatty acid synthesis), propionyl-CoA carboxylase | Avidin (in raw egg white) → binds biotin → biotin deficiency | Activated by acetyl-CoA (signals need for more OAA)",t:180},
  {q:"Which apolipoprotein activates lipoprotein lipase (LPL)?",a:"ApoB-48",b:"ApoC-II",c:"ApoA-I",d:"ApoE",ans:"B",e:"ApoC-II on the surface of chylomicrons and VLDL activates LPL on capillary endothelium, enabling hydrolysis of triglycerides to free fatty acids for tissue uptake.<br><br>Take Notes: ApoC-II deficiency → Type I hyperlipoproteinaemia (massive hypertriglyceridaemia, pancreatitis) | ApoA-I → activates LCAT (HDL maturation) | ApoB-100 → LDL receptor ligand | ApoE → remnant receptor ligand (hepatic uptake of chylomicron remnants and IDL)",t:180},
  {q:"The pentose phosphate pathway (PPP) is the ONLY source of NADPH in erythrocytes. NADPH is essential for:",a:"ATP synthesis",b:"Haemoglobin synthesis",c:"Lactate production",d:"Maintaining reduced glutathione (GSH) to prevent oxidative haemolysis",ans:"D",e:"NADPH generated by G6PD in the PPP regenerates GSH via glutathione reductase; GSH neutralises H2O2, protecting RBC membranes. G6PD deficiency → depleted GSH → oxidative haemolysis with trigger drugs.<br><br>Take Notes: G6PD deficiency → X-linked recessive, most common enzymopathy | Triggers → primaquine, dapsone, nitrofurantoin, sulphonamides, fava beans | Heinz bodies → denatured Hb | PPP also provides ribose-5-phosphate for nucleotide synthesis",t:180},
  {q:"HMG-CoA reductase (rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis) is primarily regulated by:",a:"Substrate availability (HMG-CoA levels)",b:"Feedback inhibition by intracellular cholesterol via SREBP-2",c:"Allosteric activation by ATP",d:"Covalent modification by kinases only",ans:"B",e:"Intracellular cholesterol suppresses SREBP-2 nuclear translocation, reducing HMG-CoA reductase transcription - the primary long-term regulatory mechanism. Statins competitively inhibit the enzyme, compensatorily upregulating LDL receptors.<br><br>Take Notes: Statins → competitive inhibitors of HMG-CoA reductase → reduce intracellular cholesterol → upregulate LDL receptors | Located in ER | Activated by insulin | Also regulated by covalent modification (phosphorylation by AMPK inactivates it)",t:180},
  {q:"Which amino acid is the major substrate for gluconeogenesis released from muscle during starvation?",a:"Glutamine",b:"Glycine",c:"Leucine",d:"Alanine",ans:"D",e:"Alanine is the primary gluconeogenic amino acid exported from muscle - transamination of pyruvate (from glycolysis) to alanine allows nitrogen transport to the liver, where alanine is reconverted to pyruvate for gluconeogenesis (glucose-alanine cycle).<br><br>Take Notes: Glucose-alanine cycle → carries both carbon (as pyruvate) and nitrogen (as amino group) from muscle to liver | Glutamine → primary gluconeogenic substrate from gut and kidney | Leucine → purely ketogenic (cannot contribute to gluconeogenesis)",t:180},
  {q:"Homocystinuria due to cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency is characterised by all of the following EXCEPT:",a:"Downward lens dislocation (ectopia lentis)",b:"Intellectual disability",c:"Marfanoid habitus",d:"Upward lens dislocation",ans:"D",e:"Cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency causes homocystinuria with downward and inward lens dislocation - the opposite of Marfan syndrome (which causes upward and outward lens dislocation due to fibrillin-1 defect).<br><br>Take Notes: Homocystinuria → downward lens dislocation + marfanoid habitus + intellectual disability + thromboembolism | Marfan → upward lens dislocation + arachnodactyly + aortic root dilation + NO intellectual disability | Treatment → B6 (pyridoxine) responsive in some patients",t:180},
  {q:"The enzyme responsible for converting angiotensin I to angiotensin II is:",a:"Renin",b:"Aldosterone synthase",c:"Chymase",d:"Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE/kininase II)",ans:"D",e:"ACE (kininase II) on pulmonary endothelium cleaves the C-terminal dipeptide from angiotensin I to form the active angiotensin II. ACE also degrades bradykinin - explaining ACEi-induced cough from bradykinin accumulation.<br><br>Take Notes: ACE inhibitors → block angiotensin II + bradykinin accumulation → cough | ARBs → block AT1 receptor only, no bradykinin accumulation → no cough | Chymase → alternative pathway for Ang I → Ang II in cardiac tissue (ACEi-independent)",t:180},
  {q:"The most reliable indicator of irreversible cell injury is:",a:"Mitochondrial swelling",b:"Cytoplasmic vacuolation",c:"Loss of membrane integrity with enzyme release",d:"Nuclear chromatin clumping",ans:"C",e:"Plasma membrane rupture with release of intracellular enzymes (LDH, AST, troponin) into the circulation is the hallmark of irreversible cell injury - detectable clinically as elevated serum enzymes after MI, hepatitis, etc.<br><br>Take Notes: Reversible injury → cell swelling (earliest), ER dilatation, nuclear changes | Irreversible → membrane rupture, flocculent densities in mitochondria, nuclear pyknosis/karyolysis | Serum enzymes → released only after membrane rupture (marker of cell death)",t:180},
  {q:"Caseous necrosis is most characteristic of:",a:"Ischaemic infarction",b:"Tuberculosis (granulomatous necrosis)",c:"Fat necrosis",d:"Coagulative necrosis",ans:"B",e:"Caseous necrosis (cheese-like, soft, white material) is pathognomonic of tuberculosis; it represents granulomatous necrosis where cell outlines are completely lost, unlike coagulative necrosis where cell ghosts persist.<br><br>Take Notes: Coagulative → ischaemic infarction (cell ghosts preserved) | Caseous → TB, fungi | Liquefactive → brain infarction, abscess | Fat necrosis → pancreatitis (saponification with calcium soaps) | Fibrinoid → autoimmune vasculitis",t:180},
  {q:"Which cells form the Aschoff bodies in rheumatic carditis?",a:"Neutrophils",b:"Plasma cells",c:"Anitschkow cells (modified macrophages) in a granulomatous focus",d:"Giant cells",ans:"C",e:"Aschoff bodies are pathognomonic of rheumatic carditis - granulomatous foci consisting of central fibrinoid necrosis surrounded by Anitschkow cells (caterpillar cells - macrophages with wavy chromatin) and multinucleated Aschoff giant cells.<br><br>Take Notes: Aschoff body → pathognomonic of rheumatic fever | Anitschkow cells → modified macrophages with caterpillar nuclei | Molecular mimicry → GAS M protein cross-reacts with cardiac myosin | Most common valve affected → mitral (then aortic)",t:180},
  {q:"Dystrophic calcification occurs in:",a:"Normal tissue with elevated serum calcium",b:"Necrotic or damaged tissue with normal serum calcium",c:"Metastatic deposits with hypercalcaemia",d:"Normal kidney tubules",ans:"B",e:"Dystrophic calcification occurs in dead or damaged tissue (atherosclerotic plaques, old tuberculosis foci, heart valves, tumour necrosis) with normal serum calcium and phosphate levels - it represents end-organ deposition of calcium.<br><br>Take Notes: Dystrophic → necrotic tissue + normal serum calcium | Metastatic → normal tissue + elevated serum calcium (hyperparathyroidism, hypervitaminosis D) | Pathological calcification of vessels → Monckeberg (medial) vs atherosclerosis (intimal)",t:180},
  {q:"BCL-2 overexpression in follicular lymphoma results from translocation:",a:"t(8;14)",b:"t(14;18)",c:"t(9;22)",d:"t(11;14)",ans:"B",e:"The t(14;18) translocation juxtaposes BCL-2 (chromosome 18) next to the IgH enhancer (chromosome 14), causing constitutive BCL-2 overexpression - preventing apoptosis of germinal centre B cells and allowing their accumulation.<br><br>Take Notes: t(8;14) → MYC-IgH = Burkitt lymphoma | t(14;18) → BCL-2 = follicular lymphoma | t(9;22) → BCR-ABL = CML/ALL | t(11;14) → cyclin D1 = mantle cell lymphoma | BCL-2 inhibitor → venetoclax (CLL treatment)",t:180},
  {q:"The Reed-Sternberg cell is pathognomonic of:",a:"Non-Hodgkin lymphoma",b:"Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia",c:"Classical Hodgkin lymphoma",d:"Infectious mononucleosis",ans:"C",e:"Classical Reed-Sternberg cells are large binucleate or multinucleate cells with prominent owl-eye nucleoli (CD15+, CD30+, CD45-) - pathognomonic of classical Hodgkin lymphoma when seen in appropriate cellular background.<br><br>Take Notes: RS cells → CD15+ and CD30+ (positive), CD45- (negative) | Lacunar cells → nodular sclerosis subtype | Background → eosinophils, plasma cells, lymphocytes (reactive infiltrate) | EBV → associated with mixed cellularity subtype",t:180},
  {q:"Amyloid displays which characteristic staining property?",a:"PAS positive, no birefringence",b:"Congo red staining with apple-green birefringence under polarised light",c:"Masson trichrome positive",d:"Alcian blue positive",ans:"B",e:"Amyloid is diagnosed by Congo red staining showing apple-green birefringence under polarised light, due to the cross-beta-pleated sheet structure of amyloid fibrils diffracting polarised light.<br><br>Take Notes: AL amyloid → light chains (multiple myeloma, primary) | AA amyloid → serum amyloid A (chronic inflammation: RA, TB, osteomyelitis) | ATTR → transthyretin (hereditary or senile cardiac) | Amyloid deposits → extracellular | Confirmatory → rectal or abdominal fat biopsy",t:180},
  {q:"Which tumour marker is most elevated in hepatocellular carcinoma?",a:"CEA",b:"CA 19-9",c:"AFP (alpha-fetoprotein)",d:"CA 125",ans:"C",e:"AFP is elevated in >70% of HCC cases and combined with characteristic CT/MRI features (arterial enhancement + venous washout) is diagnostic without biopsy in cirrhotic patients with lesions >1cm.<br><br>Take Notes: AFP also elevated in → yolk sac tumours (non-seminomatous GCT), hepatoblastoma, pregnancy | AFP >200 ng/mL + typical imaging in cirrhosis → HCC diagnosis without biopsy | CA 19-9 → pancreatic cancer (not specific) | CEA → colorectal cancer monitoring",t:180},
  {q:"Wire-loop lesions on renal biopsy are characteristic of:",a:"Post-streptococcal GN",b:"IgA nephropathy",c:"Lupus nephritis class IV (diffuse proliferative)",d:"Minimal change disease",ans:"C",e:"Wire-loop lesions represent massive subendothelial immune complex deposits in lupus nephritis class IV - the most severe form, causing 'full house' immunofluorescence (IgG, IgA, IgM, C3, C1q).<br><br>Take Notes: Wire-loop → subendothelial deposits in lupus nephritis | Post-strep GN → subepithelial humps (hump sign) on EM, granular IF | IgA nephropathy → mesangial IgA deposits | Minimal change → no deposits on EM | Membranous → subepithelial (spike and dome pattern)",t:180},
  {q:"The cell type most commonly responsible for liver fibrosis (cirrhosis) is:",a:"Hepatocytes",b:"Kupffer cells",c:"Hepatic stellate cells (Ito cells)",d:"Sinusoidal endothelial cells",ans:"C",e:"Hepatic stellate cells (Ito cells) are the primary effector cells of liver fibrosis - when activated by TGF-beta from Kupffer cells and injured hepatocytes, they transform into myofibroblasts producing collagen type I and III.<br><br>Take Notes: Stellate cells → normally store vitamin A in perisinusoidal space | Activated by TGF-beta → myofibroblast → collagen deposition | Anti-fibrotic targets → TGF-beta pathway, stellate cell activation | Kupffer cells → resident liver macrophages, activated first",t:180},
  {q:"Psammoma bodies are characteristically found in which tumour?",a:"Medullary carcinoma of thyroid",b:"Papillary carcinoma of thyroid",c:"Follicular carcinoma of thyroid",d:"Anaplastic carcinoma of thyroid",ans:"B",e:"Psammoma bodies (concentric calcified laminations) are characteristic of papillary thyroid carcinoma, along with Orphan Annie eye nuclei (ground-glass nuclei) and nuclear grooves.<br><br>Take Notes: Psammoma bodies also in → papillary serous ovarian carcinoma, meningioma, papillary renal cell carcinoma | Papillary thyroid Ca → most common thyroid malignancy, excellent prognosis, spreads via lymphatics | Medullary thyroid Ca → calcitonin-producing (C cells), associated with MEN2A/2B",t:180},
  {q:"The mechanism of Clostridium difficile colitis involves:",a:"Direct mucosal invasion",b:"Toxin A (enterotoxin) and Toxin B (cytotoxin) disrupting tight junctions and actin cytoskeleton",c:"Superantigen-mediated T cell activation",d:"LPS-mediated endotoxaemia",ans:"B",e:"C. difficile toxins A and B inactivate Rho GTPases (by glucosylation), disrupting actin cytoskeleton and tight junctions, causing mucosal inflammation, pseudomembrane formation, and fluid secretion.<br><br>Take Notes: Risk factors → antibiotics (clindamycin, cephalosporins, quinolones), PPI use, hospitalisation, elderly | Treatment → metronidazole (mild) or oral vancomycin (severe) or fidaxomicin | Recurrence → common; faecal microbiota transplant for recurrent CDI",t:180},
  {q:"The hallmark of Hodgkin lymphoma (nodular sclerosis subtype) on histology is:",a:"Diffuse infiltration without nodule formation",b:"Collagen bands dividing tissue into nodules with lacunar Reed-Sternberg cells",c:"Predominance of plasma cells",d:"Complete effacement of nodal architecture by small lymphocytes",ans:"B",e:"Nodular sclerosis Hodgkin lymphoma (most common subtype, ~70%) is characterised by fibrous collagen bands dividing the node into nodules containing lacunar cells (RS variants with pale cytoplasm retraction artefact).<br><br>Take Notes: Nodular sclerosis → most common, bimodal age (young adults + elderly), female predominance, mediastinal involvement | Mixed cellularity → EBV-associated, intermediate prognosis | Lymphocyte predominant → best prognosis | Lymphocyte depleted → worst prognosis",t:180},
  {q:"The Ziehl-Neelsen stain identifies acid-fast organisms because mycobacteria:",a:"Have a thin peptidoglycan layer",b:"Have a thick waxy mycolic acid cell wall that resists decolorisation with acid-alcohol",c:"Produce spores that resist staining",d:"Are gram-positive with extra thick peptidoglycan",ans:"B",e:"Mycobacterial cell walls contain high concentrations of mycolic acid (long-chain fatty acids) which bind carbol fuchsin and resist decolorisation by acid-alcohol - the basis of acid-fastness and ZN staining.<br><br>Take Notes: ZN stain → red bacilli on blue background | Other acid-fast organisms → Nocardia (weakly), Cryptosporidium (modified ZN) | Mantoux test → type IV hypersensitivity (48-72h) | BCG → derived from M. bovis | MTB → strict aerobe, obligate intracellular in macrophages",t:180},
  {q:"Pseudomonas aeruginosa produces which toxin that inactivates EF-2 (same mechanism as diphtheria toxin)?",a:"Exotoxin S",b:"Exotoxin A",c:"Pyocyanin",d:"Alginate",ans:"B",e:"Pseudomonas exotoxin A ADP-ribosylates and inactivates EF-2 (elongation factor 2), halting protein synthesis - the identical mechanism to diphtheria toxin, but encoded by different genes.<br><br>Take Notes: Pseudomonas → common in burns, CF, immunocompromised, ICU | Treatment → anti-pseudomonal beta-lactam (piperacillin-tazobactam, ceftazidime) + aminoglycoside | Pyocyanin → blue-green pigment, generates superoxide | Alginate → biofilm formation",t:180},
  {q:"The most common pathogen causing osteomyelitis in sickle cell disease patients is:",a:"Staphylococcus aureus",b:"Streptococcus pneumoniae",c:"Salmonella species",d:"Pseudomonas aeruginosa",ans:"C",e:"Sickle cell patients are functionally asplenic, impairing clearance of encapsulated organisms and Salmonella; furthermore, Salmonella has a predilection for infarcted bone in sickle cell disease - the combination makes it the most common pathogen in this context.<br><br>Take Notes: General osteomyelitis → Staph aureus most common in all other situations | Sickle cell → Salmonella (but Staph aureus still occurs) | Neonates → GBS + Staph aureus + gram-negatives | IV drug users → Pseudomonas + Staph aureus",t:180},
  {q:"Helicobacter pylori is associated with all of the following EXCEPT:",a:"Peptic ulcer disease",b:"Gastric adenocarcinoma",c:"MALT lymphoma",d:"Oesophageal adenocarcinoma",ans:"D",e:"H. pylori is strongly associated with duodenal and gastric ulcers, gastric adenocarcinoma (non-cardia), and MALT lymphoma (which may regress with H. pylori eradication). Oesophageal adenocarcinoma is associated with GORD and Barrett's oesophagus, not H. pylori.<br><br>Take Notes: H. pylori → type B gastritis (antral) | Virulence factors → CagA (most virulent strains), VacA, urease | Eradication → PPI + clarithromycin + amoxicillin (14 days) | Test of cure → urea breath test or stool antigen ≥4 weeks post-treatment",t:180},
  {q:"The complement pathway activation in post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is primarily via:",a:"Classical pathway (antibody-mediated)",b:"Lectin pathway",c:"Alternative pathway (C3 activation without antibody)",d:"Both classical and alternative equally",ans:"C",e:"Post-streptococcal GN primarily activates complement via the alternative pathway - elevated ASO titre with markedly reduced C3 (but normal C4) is the classic serological pattern.<br><br>Take Notes: Low C3 + normal C4 → alternative pathway (PSGN, MPGN) | Low C3 + low C4 → classical pathway (SLE, cryoglobulinaemia) | PSGN → develops 1-3 weeks post-pharyngitis, 2-6 weeks post-impetigo | Nephritic syndrome → haematuria, proteinuria, hypertension, oedema, oliguria",t:180},
  {q:"Clostridium tetani produces tetanospasmin, which acts by:",a:"Blocking ACh release at NMJ",b:"Cleaving VAMP/synaptobrevin in inhibitory interneurons of the spinal cord, causing spastic paralysis",c:"ADP-ribosylating Gi proteins",d:"Directly depolarising motor neurons",ans:"B",e:"Tetanospasmin cleaves VAMP/synaptobrevin (SNARE protein) in inhibitory glycinergic and GABAergic interneurons of the spinal cord, blocking inhibitory neurotransmission and causing uncontrolled motor firing (spastic paralysis, trismus, risus sardonicus).<br><br>Take Notes: Tetanus → spastic paralysis (vs botulinum → flaccid) | Both cleave SNARE proteins, but different targets and locations | Treatment → tetanus immunoglobulin + wound debridement + metronidazole + diazepam | Toxoid → immunisation via routine vaccination",t:180},
  {q:"Which virus is associated with Kaposi sarcoma in AIDS patients?",a:"EBV",b:"CMV",c:"HHV-8 (KSHV)",d:"HPV-16",ans:"C",e:"HHV-8 (Kaposi sarcoma-associated herpesvirus/KSHV) is the causative agent of all four forms of Kaposi sarcoma (epidemic/AIDS-related, classic, endemic African, iatrogenic) - it is always identified in tumour cells.<br><br>Take Notes: KS → spindle-cell tumour of vascular/lymphatic endothelium | AIDS-KS → CD4 <200, purple/violaceous skin lesions, mucosal involvement | Treatment → ART (most important) + chemotherapy if severe | KSHV also causes → primary effusion lymphoma, multicentric Castleman disease",t:180},
  {q:"Sensitivity of a test is defined as:",a:"TP/(TP+FP)",b:"TN/(TN+FP)",c:"TP/(TP+FN)",d:"TN/(TN+FN)",ans:"C",e:"Sensitivity = TP/(TP+FN) - the proportion of truly diseased who test positive. High sensitivity minimises false negatives; SnNout (high Sensitivity rules out disease).<br><br>Take Notes: Specificity = TN/(TN+FP) | PPV = TP/(TP+FP) - varies with prevalence | NPV = TN/(TN+FN) - varies with prevalence | Sensitivity + specificity are intrinsic test properties (fixed)",t:180},
  {q:"Relative risk (RR) can be directly calculated from which study design?",a:"Case-control study",b:"Cross-sectional study",c:"Cohort study",d:"Ecological study",ans:"C",e:"RR = incidence in exposed / incidence in unexposed - requires knowledge of incidence in both groups, which is only directly measurable in cohort studies (which follow groups from exposure to outcome).<br><br>Take Notes: Case-control → calculates OR (approximates RR when disease is rare) | Cross-sectional → prevalence ratio | Cohort → can calculate RR, AR, incidence | RCT → relative risk reduction (RRR), absolute risk reduction (ARR), NNT",t:180},
  {q:"The infant mortality rate is defined as:",a:"Deaths under 5 years per 1000 live births",b:"Deaths under 1 year per 1000 live births in the same year",c:"Deaths in neonatal period per 1000 live births",d:"Stillbirths plus neonatal deaths per 1000 total births",ans:"B",e:"IMR = deaths under 1 year of age per 1000 live births in the same year - the most widely used indicator of socioeconomic development, maternal health, and health service quality.<br><br>Take Notes: Neonatal mortality rate → deaths <28 days per 1000 live births | Perinatal mortality rate → stillbirths + early neonatal deaths (0-7 days) per 1000 total births | Pakistan IMR → ~55-60 per 1000 (well above global target of <12)",t:180},
  {q:"Type I error (alpha error) in a clinical trial means:",a:"Failing to detect a true treatment effect",b:"Rejecting a true null hypothesis (false positive result)",c:"Including a biased sample",d:"Loss to follow-up bias",ans:"B",e:"Type I error = rejecting H₀ when it is actually true - concluding there IS an effect when there isn't. The probability of Type I error is α (typically set at 0.05 = 5% chance of false positive).<br><br>Take Notes: Type I (α) → false positive | Type II (β) → false negative | Power = 1 − β | Reducing α → more stringent significance threshold | Bonferroni correction → for multiple comparisons, divides α by number of tests",t:180},
  {q:"Herd immunity threshold for measles (R₀ ≈ 15) is approximately:",a:"70%",b:"80%",c:"93%",d:"99%",ans:"C",e:"HIT = 1 − (1/R₀) = 1 − 1/15 = 93%. Measles has one of the highest R₀ values of any infectious disease, requiring very high vaccine coverage to interrupt transmission.<br><br>Take Notes: HIT = 1 − 1/R₀ | Measles → HIT 93-95% (hence outbreaks with <95% coverage) | Polio → HIT ~85% | Smallpox → HIT ~85% (eradicated at lower coverage due to ring vaccination) | COVID-19 → HIT 60-80% (varies by variant)",t:180},
  {q:"Attribution of causality in epidemiology - which Bradford Hill criterion is the ONLY mandatory one?",a:"Strength of association",b:"Biological plausibility",c:"Temporality",d:"Consistency",ans:"C",e:"Temporality - exposure must precede outcome - is the only logically necessary criterion for causation. All other Bradford Hill criteria are supportive evidence but none is individually mandatory.<br><br>Take Notes: Temporality → only mandatory criterion | Strength → large RR supports (but not required) | Biological plausibility → depends on existing knowledge | Hill's criteria → guidelines not rules (no threshold required)",t:180},
  {q:"The DALY (disability-adjusted life year) is calculated as:",a:"Years of life lost (YLL) only",b:"Years lived with disability (YLD) only",c:"YLL + YLD",d:"Life expectancy minus healthy years",ans:"C",e:"DALY = YLL (years of life lost to premature death) + YLD (years lived with disability weighted by severity) - one DALY represents one year of healthy life lost, measuring disease burden combining mortality and morbidity.<br><br>Take Notes: QALY → quality-adjusted life year (health economics, benefits perspective) | DALY → disease burden (loss perspective) | Leading DALYs globally → cardiovascular disease, neonatal conditions, COPD | Pakistan → infectious diseases + perinatal conditions still major contributors",t:180},
  {q:"Which vaccine is contraindicated in immunocompromised patients?",a:"Inactivated influenza vaccine",b:"Hepatitis B vaccine",c:"BCG vaccine",d:"Meningococcal conjugate vaccine",ans:"C",e:"BCG is a live attenuated vaccine; in immunocompromised patients (HIV with low CD4, on immunosuppressants, primary immunodeficiency), live vaccines can cause disseminated infection (BCG-itis) and are contraindicated.<br><br>Take Notes: Live vaccines → BCG, OPV, MMR, varicella, yellow fever, oral typhoid → contraindicated in immunocompromised | Inactivated, subunit, toxoid vaccines → safe in immunocompromised (may have reduced efficacy) | HIV → MMR can be given if CD4 >200",t:180},
  {q:"An ecological fallacy occurs when:",a:"There is random error in a study",b:"Group-level associations are incorrectly applied to individuals",c:"A confounder is not controlled for",d:"The study lacks external validity",ans:"B",e:"The ecological fallacy is concluding that group-level (population) associations apply to individuals - groups may differ in one variable while individuals within those groups have the opposite relationship.<br><br>Take Notes: Ecological studies → group-level data, hypothesis generation only | Cannot control individual confounders | Example → country with high fat intake + high heart disease ≠ individuals with high fat intake → heart disease | Simpson's paradox → related phenomenon where aggregate data misleads",t:180},
  {q:"Which stage in Kübler-Ross model is characterised by attempts to negotiate with God or fate to postpone death?",a:"Denial",b:"Anger",c:"Bargaining",d:"Depression",ans:"C",e:"The bargaining stage involves the patient attempting to negotiate (e.g., 'if I recover, I will...') as a psychological attempt to delay the inevitable - it often involves magical thinking and guilt.<br><br>Take Notes: Kübler-Ross stages → Denial → Anger → Bargaining → Depression → Acceptance | Stages are non-linear, variable, may not all occur | Apply to any significant loss, not just terminal illness | Do not force patients through stages",t:180},
  {q:"Transference in a doctor-patient relationship refers to:",a:"The doctor's emotional response to the patient",b:"The patient unconsciously transferring feelings from past relationships onto the doctor",c:"Transfer of medical information between specialties",d:"Referring patients to specialists",ans:"B",e:"Transference is the unconscious redirection of feelings from past significant relationships (parents, authority figures) onto the current relationship with the doctor - it can be positive (idealisation, trust) or negative (hostility, distrust).<br><br>Take Notes: Countertransference → doctor's unconscious response to patient | Positive transference → idealisation, excessive trust | Negative → hostility, suspicion | Management → self-awareness, supervision, maintaining professional boundaries",t:180},
  {q:"The Health Belief Model predicts health behaviour based on all EXCEPT:",a:"Perceived susceptibility to disease",b:"Perceived barriers to action",c:"External locus of control as primary predictor",d:"Perceived benefits of health action",ans:"C",e:"The HBM (Rosenstock 1966) predicts health behaviour from: perceived susceptibility, perceived severity, perceived benefits, perceived barriers, cues to action, and self-efficacy. External locus of control is NOT a component of the HBM.<br><br>Take Notes: HBM → four core perceptions + cues to action + self-efficacy | External locus of control → belongs to Rotter's concept, relevant to adherence but not part of HBM | Limitation → does not account for habit, social norms, or emotions",t:180},
  {q:"Secondary gain in illness behaviour is best exemplified by:",a:"Symptom production reducing unconscious anxiety",b:"Patient receiving increased attention and exemption from responsibilities due to illness",c:"Biological mechanism generating symptoms",d:"Neurological basis of conversion",ans:"B",e:"Secondary gain refers to tangible external benefits obtained through maintaining the sick role (attention, compensation, role exemption) - it reinforces illness behaviour regardless of initial cause and does not imply conscious malingering.<br><br>Take Notes: Primary gain → internal psychological benefit (symptom reduces unconscious conflict) | Secondary gain → external social benefits | Tertiary gain → benefits to carers or the system | Secondary gain ≠ malingering (conscious deception for external gain)",t:180},
  {q:"The sick role concept (Parsons) includes all EXCEPT:",a:"Patient is exempt from normal social responsibilities",b:"Patient has an obligation to seek competent medical help",c:"Patient is held morally responsible for being ill",d:"Patient has the right to be cared for",ans:"C",e:"Parsons' sick role specifically states that patients are NOT responsible for their illness - they have legitimate exemption from duties, an obligation to seek care, and the right to be cared for, but must cooperate with treatment to regain normal functioning.<br><br>Take Notes: Parsons sick role criticisms → does not apply to chronic illness, disability, or mental illness | Fails to account for healthcare-seeking behaviour variation | Relevant to JCAT BSc exams | Sick role rights → exemption from duties + right to care | Obligations → seek treatment + cooperate",t:180},
  {q:"The mechanism of action of metronidazole is:",a:"Inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis",b:"Inhibits protein synthesis at the 30S ribosomal subunit",c:"Reduced to toxic free radical intermediates that damage DNA of anaerobic organisms",d:"Inhibits DNA gyrase",ans:"C",e:"Metronidazole is reduced by anaerobic organisms (which have low redox potential) to toxic radical intermediates that directly damage DNA, making it selectively active against anaerobes and certain protozoa.<br><br>Take Notes: Active against → Bacteroides, Clostridium, H. pylori (component of triple therapy), Giardia, Entamoeba, Trichomonas | Disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol | Causes peripheral neuropathy (long-term use) | Safe in 2nd and 3rd trimester (avoid 1st trimester)",t:180},
  {q:"Which antibiotic class inhibits bacterial protein synthesis at the 50S ribosomal subunit?",a:"Aminoglycosides",b:"Tetracyclines",c:"Macrolides and chloramphenicol",d:"Quinolones",ans:"C",e:"Macrolides (erythromycin, azithromycin, clarithromycin) and chloramphenicol bind the 50S ribosomal subunit, blocking translocation and peptide elongation respectively.<br><br>Take Notes: 30S inhibitors → aminoglycosides (bactericidal) + tetracyclines (bacteriostatic) | 50S inhibitors → macrolides, chloramphenicol, clindamycin, linezolid | DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II) → quinolones | Cell wall → beta-lactams + vancomycin + glycopeptides",t:180},
  {q:"Which drug is used for reversal of non-depolarising neuromuscular blockade?",a:"Succinylcholine",b:"Atracurium",c:"Neostigmine (with glycopyrrolate)",d:"Dantrolene",ans:"C",e:"Neostigmine inhibits acetylcholinesterase, increasing synaptic ACh concentration to compete with the non-depolarising blocker at the NMJ. Glycopyrrolate (or atropine) is co-administered to block muscarinic side effects of neostigmine (bradycardia, salivation).<br><br>Take Notes: Sugammadex → selective binding of rocuronium/vecuronium (newer reversal agent, no muscarinic effects) | Dantrolene → treats malignant hyperthermia (not NMJ reversal) | Succinylcholine → depolarising NMJ blocker (not reversed by neostigmine)",t:180},
  {q:"The antidote for paracetamol (acetaminophen) overdose is:",a:"Fomepizole",b:"N-acetylcysteine (NAC)",c:"Activated charcoal alone",d:"Flumazenil",ans:"B",e:"NAC replenishes intracellular glutathione, allowing conjugation and detoxification of the toxic NAPQI metabolite responsible for centrilobular hepatic necrosis. It must be given within 8-10 hours of overdose for maximum efficacy.<br><br>Take Notes: NAPQI → produced by CYP2E1 (and CYP3A4) | Depletes glutathione → covalent binding to hepatocytes → necrosis | NAC → acts as glutathione precursor + direct antioxidant | Rumack-Matthew nomogram → guides NAC administration based on serum paracetamol level vs time",t:180},
  {q:"Warfarin works by inhibiting:",a:"Factor Xa directly",b:"Thrombin directly",c:"Vitamin K epoxide reductase (VKOR), preventing gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors",d:"Platelet aggregation",ans:"C",e:"Warfarin inhibits VKOR (vitamin K epoxide reductase complex 1/VKORC1), preventing regeneration of reduced vitamin K needed to gamma-carboxylate factors II, VII, IX, X, and proteins C and S.<br><br>Take Notes: Factor VII → shortest half-life, falls first (PT/INR elevated earliest) | Protein C shortest half-life → initial prothrombotic state (warfarin skin necrosis in protein C deficiency) | Antidote → vitamin K + PCC (prothrombin complex concentrate) for urgent reversal",t:180},
  {q:"Which LMWH is most commonly used clinically?",a:"Fondaparinux",b:"Dalteparin",c:"Enoxaparin",d:"Tinzaparin",ans:"C",e:"Enoxaparin (Clexane) is the most widely used LMWH globally, dosed at 1 mg/kg BD or 1.5 mg/kg once daily for treatment, and 40 mg once daily for prophylaxis. It has predictable pharmacokinetics allowing fixed dosing without routine monitoring.<br><br>Take Notes: LMWH → primarily inhibit factor Xa (anti-Xa:anti-IIa ratio >2:1) | Fondaparinux → pure factor Xa inhibitor (pentasaccharide, synthetic) | Dose adjust in renal impairment (reduced clearance) | LMWH does not cross placenta → safe in pregnancy",t:180},
  {q:"The mechanism of aspirin's antiplatelet effect is:",a:"Blocking ADP receptors on platelets reversibly",b:"Irreversibly acetylating COX-1 in platelets, permanently inhibiting thromboxane A2 synthesis",c:"Blocking GP IIb/IIIa receptors",d:"Phosphodiesterase inhibition increasing cAMP",ans:"B",e:"Aspirin irreversibly acetylates COX-1 (and COX-2) - since platelets lack nuclei and cannot synthesise new COX, the antiplatelet effect lasts the platelet lifespan (7-10 days). Thromboxane A2 promotes platelet aggregation and vasoconstriction.<br><br>Take Notes: Stop aspirin 7-10 days before surgery (platelet lifespan) | Clopidogrel → irreversibly blocks P2Y12 (ADP receptor) | GP IIb/IIIa inhibitors → abciximab, eptifibatide, tirofiban | Dipyridamole → PDE inhibitor + adenosine uptake inhibition",t:180},
  {q:"Morphine's primary analgesic mechanism is:",a:"COX inhibition",b:"NMDA receptor blockade",c:"Agonism at mu-opioid receptors, reducing cAMP and opening K+ channels",d:"Serotonin reuptake inhibition",ans:"C",e:"Morphine activates mu-opioid receptors (Gi-coupled) reducing adenylyl cyclase activity (decreasing cAMP), opening K+ channels (hyperpolarising neurons), and closing Ca2+ channels - inhibiting pain signal transmission in dorsal horn and supraspinal sites.<br><br>Take Notes: Opioid receptors → mu (analgesia, respiratory depression, constipation), kappa (spinal analgesia, dysphoria), delta (analgesia) | Naloxone → competitive mu-antagonist (opioid reversal) | Constipation → peripherally acting opioid antagonist (methylnaltrexone) for opioid-induced constipation",t:180},
  {q:"Which drug causes acute dystonia as an adverse effect?",a:"Ondansetron",b:"Metoclopramide (D2 receptor antagonist)",c:"Atropine",d:"Midazolam",ans:"B",e:"Metoclopramide and other D2 antagonists (haloperidol, prochlorperazine) cause acute dystonic reactions (torticollis, oculogyric crisis, trismus) by blocking dopamine receptors in the basal ganglia - particularly in young patients and with high doses.<br><br>Take Notes: Acute dystonia → treat with procyclidine (anticholinergic) or benztropine | Tardive dyskinesia → long-term D2 blockade | Neuroleptic malignant syndrome → severe hyperthermia, rigidity, autonomic instability from D2 blockade | Metoclopramide → also 5-HT4 agonist (prokinetic) + 5-HT3 antagonist (antiemetic)",t:180},
  {q:"Which beta-blocker has intrinsic sympathomimetic activity (ISA) and is therefore less likely to cause bradycardia at rest?",a:"Atenolol",b:"Metoprolol",c:"Propranolol",d:"Pindolol",ans:"D",e:"Pindolol is a non-selective beta-blocker with ISA - it partially agonises beta-receptors at rest, preventing excessive bradycardia at rest while still antagonising catecholamine-driven tachycardia on exertion.<br><br>Take Notes: ISA (partial agonist activity) → pindolol, oxprenolol, acebutolol | Advantage → less resting bradycardia, less cold extremities | Disadvantage → may not be cardioprotective post-MI (ISA blocks benefit) | Carvedilol → beta + alpha-1 blocker (used in heart failure)",t:180},
  {q:"The diuretic of choice for nephrotic syndrome with oedema is:",a:"Acetazolamide",b:"Spironolactone",c:"Furosemide",d:"Hydrochlorothiazide",ans:"C",e:"Furosemide is the first-line diuretic for nephrotic oedema - the powerful natriuresis of loop diuretics overcomes the low oncotic pressure-driven oedema. High doses may be required as hypoalbuminaemia reduces drug delivery to tubular lumen.<br><br>Take Notes: Nephrotic syndrome → heavy proteinuria + hypoalbuminaemia + oedema + hyperlipidaemia | Spironolactone → useful in cirrhotic ascites | Thiazides → less potent, used for mild oedema or in combination | Furosemide resistance → albumin infusion helps deliver drug to tubule",t:180},
  {q:"Fluconazole is preferred over amphotericin B for cryptococcal meningitis consolidation therapy because:",a:"It is more potent",b:"It can be given orally with excellent CNS penetration and lower toxicity",c:"It has a broader spectrum of activity",d:"It does not require monitoring",ans:"B",e:"Fluconazole has excellent oral bioavailability (~90%) and CSF penetration; it is used for consolidation (following induction with amphotericin B + flucytosine) and maintenance therapy of cryptococcal meningitis in HIV patients.<br><br>Take Notes: Cryptococcal meningitis protocol → induction: liposomal amphotericin B + flucytosine (2 weeks) → consolidation: fluconazole 400mg (8 weeks) → maintenance: fluconazole 200mg (until CD4 >200 on ART) | Fluconazole mechanism → inhibits CYP51 (lanosterol demethylase), blocking ergosterol synthesis",t:180},
  {q:"Which drug combination is used as first-line treatment for H. pylori eradication?",a:"Amoxicillin + metronidazole only",b:"PPI + clarithromycin + amoxicillin (triple therapy for 14 days)",c:"Tetracycline + metronidazole only",d:"Ranitidine + clarithromycin",ans:"B",e:"Standard first-line H. pylori eradication: PPI (omeprazole/pantoprazole) + clarithromycin + amoxicillin for 14 days achieves 70-85% eradication when clarithromycin resistance is <20% in the region.<br><br>Take Notes: Clarithromycin resistance >20% → use bismuth quadruple therapy (PPI + bismuth + tetracycline + metronidazole) | Test of cure → urea breath test or stool antigen ≥4 weeks post-treatment | Sequential therapy → increasing use in high-resistance areas",t:180},
  {q:"Phenytoin toxicity first manifests as:",a:"Cardiac arrhythmias",b:"Bone marrow suppression",c:"Nystagmus, ataxia, and diplopia (cerebellar/vestibular signs)",d:"Rash only",ans:"C",e:"Phenytoin toxicity follows a predictable dose-dependent progression: nystagmus (first sign) → diplopia and ataxia → mental status changes → coma. This reflects its cerebellar and vestibular toxicity before cortical effects.<br><br>Take Notes: Chronic phenytoin toxicity → gingival hyperplasia, hirsutism, acne, coarsening of facial features, peripheral neuropathy, folate deficiency, osteomalacia | Saturable kinetics (zero-order at therapeutic levels) → small dose increases → large plasma level rise | Teratogenic → fetal hydantoin syndrome",t:180},
  {q:"The class of drugs that competitively inhibits aldosterone receptors at the collecting duct is:",a:"Loop diuretics",b:"Thiazide diuretics",c:"Potassium-sparing diuretics (spironolactone group)",d:"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors",ans:"C",e:"Spironolactone and eplerenone competitively block aldosterone (mineralocorticoid) receptors in the principal cells of the collecting duct, preventing ENaC upregulation - reducing sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion.<br><br>Take Notes: Spironolactone → non-selective (also blocks androgen receptors → gynaecomastia, impotence) | Eplerenone → selective mineralocorticoid antagonist (no anti-androgenic effects) | Hyperkalaemia → most dangerous side effect | Uses → heart failure (RALES, EMPHASIS trials), hyperaldosteronism, ascites",t:180},
  {q:"Atorvastatin differs from simvastatin pharmacokinetically because atorvastatin:",a:"Has shorter half-life and is given in the morning",b:"Is a prodrug requiring hepatic activation",c:"Has a long half-life (14 hours) and active metabolites allowing evening or morning dosing",d:"Is water-soluble and does not enter peripheral tissues",ans:"C",e:"Atorvastatin has a long half-life (~14 hours) with active hydroxyl metabolites, providing 24-hour HMG-CoA reductase inhibition regardless of dosing time - unlike short-acting statins (simvastatin, lovastatin) which should be taken at night when cholesterol synthesis peaks.<br><br>Take Notes: Rosuvastatin → longest half-life (~20h), most potent per mg | Simvastatin/lovastatin → prodrugs (acid active form) | Hydrophilic statins → rosuvastatin, pravastatin (less myopathy risk) | All statins → CYP3A4 except rosuvastatin (CYP2C9) and pravastatin (non-CYP)",t:180},
  {q:"A 65-year-old male with type 2 diabetes on metformin is scheduled for elective colorectal surgery. Metformin should be withheld:",a:"Only if eGFR <30 mL/min before surgery",b:"48 hours before surgery and restarted when oral intake resumes and renal function confirmed normal",c:"Only during general anaesthesia",d:"Never needs to be withheld for elective surgery",ans:"B",e:"Metformin is withheld 48 hours before major surgery due to risk of lactic acidosis from reduced renal clearance under physiological stress (dehydration, contrast agents, hypotension) - restarted when eating normally and renal function confirmed stable.<br><br>Take Notes: Metformin lactic acidosis → rare but fatal; caused by Complex I inhibition | Also withheld before IV contrast | SGLT-2 inhibitors → withheld 3-4 days pre-surgery (risk of euglycaemic DKA) | Insulin → continue (reduced dose) with glucose monitoring perioperatively",t:180},
  {q:"The most important physiological principle underlying ERAS (Enhanced Recovery After Surgery) protocols is:",a:"Prolonged fasting to reduce aspiration risk",b:"Aggressive IV fluid administration to ensure positive fluid balance",c:"Minimising the surgical stress response by multimodal interventions across all perioperative phases",d:"Replacing all oral analgesia with epidural anaesthesia",ans:"C",e:"ERAS protocols reduce morbidity and hospital stay by attenuating the neuroendocrine surgical stress response through pre-, intra-, and post-operative interventions - including carbohydrate loading, minimally invasive surgery, goal-directed fluid therapy, and multimodal analgesia.<br><br>Take Notes: Key ERAS elements → carbohydrate drink 2h pre-op, avoid prolonged fasting, thoracic epidural/regional anaesthesia, zero-balance fluids, early mobilisation and feeding, avoid drains/NG tubes | Developed by Henrik Kehlet",t:180},
  {q:"ASA physical status classification ASA III describes:",a:"A normal healthy patient",b:"A patient with mild systemic disease",c:"A patient with severe systemic disease that is NOT incapacitating",d:"A patient with incapacitating systemic disease that is a constant threat to life",ans:"C",e:"ASA III patients have severe but controlled systemic disease (e.g., poorly controlled DM, COPD, BMI ≥40, active hepatitis, alcohol dependence, end-stage renal disease on dialysis, premature infant <60 weeks) - the disease limits function but is not immediately life-threatening.<br><br>Take Notes: ASA I → healthy | ASA II → mild controlled disease | ASA III → severe, not incapacitating | ASA IV → incapacitating, constant threat | ASA V → moribund, not expected to survive 24h | Suffix E → emergency surgery",t:180},
  {q:"In a patient on long-term steroids (prednisolone 10 mg/day) undergoing major abdominal surgery, perioperative steroid management should include:",a:"No additional steroids (continue usual dose only)",b:"Discontinue steroids 24 hours preoperatively",c:"Hydrocortisone 100 mg IV at induction followed by 50 mg 6-8 hourly for 24-48 hours",d:"Double oral dose the morning of surgery only",ans:"C",e:"Patients on chronic steroids have suppressed HPA axis and cannot mount the normal cortisol surge (10-fold increase) in response to surgical stress - hydrocortisone stress dosing prevents potentially fatal Addisonian crisis intraoperatively and postoperatively.<br><br>Take Notes: Major surgery → 100 mg hydrocortisone IV at induction + 50 mg 6-8 hourly 24-48h | Minor surgery → 50 mg hydrocortisone at induction | Equivalent doses → hydrocortisone 20 mg = prednisolone 5 mg = dexamethasone 0.75 mg | Taper to maintenance as recovery progresses",t:180},
  {q:"Which parameter BEST reflects tissue oxygen delivery adequacy in a post-operative patient?",a:"SpO2 ≥95%",b:"Serum lactate level and lactate clearance",c:"Urine output >0.5 mL/kg/hr alone",d:"Mean arterial pressure >65 mmHg alone",ans:"B",e:"Serum lactate reflects global tissue hypoperfusion - elevated lactate (>2 mmol/L) with impaired clearance indicates anaerobic metabolism despite potentially normal vital signs. Lactate-guided resuscitation in sepsis reduces mortality.<br><br>Take Notes: Lactate >4 mmol/L = severe tissue hypoperfusion (major resuscitation target) | Lactate clearance ≥10% per hour → improving perfusion | Normal lactate with high MAP + UO does not exclude tissue hypoperfusion (cryptic shock) | ScvO2 <70% → also marker of inadequate O2 delivery",t:180},
  {q:"The Waterlow score in surgical patients assesses risk of:",a:"Deep vein thrombosis",b:"Pressure sore (decubitus ulcer) development",c:"Postoperative chest infection",d:"Anastomotic dehiscence",ans:"B",e:"The Waterlow score assesses pressure sore risk by evaluating body mass, skin condition, continence, mobility, age, sex, nutrition, neurological deficit, and surgery type - guiding pressure area care and mattress selection.<br><br>Take Notes: Waterlow >10 = at risk; >15 = high risk; >20 = very high risk | Braden scale → alternative tool (lower score = higher risk) | Norton scale → original pressure sore risk tool | Prevention → repositioning 2-hourly, pressure-relieving mattress, nutritional support",t:180},
  {q:"A patient undergoes sigmoid colectomy. On day 5 they develop swinging pyrexia with a WBC of 18 × 10⁹/L. CT shows a 5 cm collection in the pelvis. The BEST management is:",a:"IV antibiotics alone for 2 weeks",b:"Laparoscopic re-exploration",c:"CT-guided percutaneous drainage + IV antibiotics",d:"Open re-exploration immediately",ans:"C",e:"Post-operative intra-abdominal collections accessible percutaneously are best managed by CT-guided drainage combined with antibiotics - this is less invasive than re-laparotomy, controls sepsis effectively, and avoids the morbidity of early re-exploration.<br><br>Take Notes: CT-guided drainage → suitable for accessible, unilocular collections | Anastomotic leak → may require re-operation if diffuse peritonitis | Indications for laparotomy → haemodynamic instability, faecal peritonitis, inaccessible collection | Early enteral nutrition reduces risk of post-operative collections",t:180},
  {q:"The most common cause of postoperative fever in the first 24-48 hours is:",a:"Wound infection",b:"Urinary tract infection",c:"Pulmonary atelectasis",d:"Deep vein thrombosis",ans:"C",e:"Atelectasis from retained secretions and reduced deep breathing under anaesthesia causes fever in the first 48 hours - 'wind' is the earliest W in the surgical mnemonic. Physiotherapy and incentive spirometry are the primary treatments.<br><br>Take Notes: Surgical fever mnemonic 5 Ws → Wind (atelectasis, 1-2d), Water (UTI, 3-5d), Wound (5-7d), Walking (DVT, 5+d), Wonder drugs (drug fever, anytime) | Wound infection → rarely presents before day 5 | Day 1-2 fever → almost always atelectasis or physiological stress response",t:180},
  {q:"Blood transfusion in elective surgery is indicated when haemoglobin falls below which threshold in a symptomatic patient with cardiovascular disease?",a:"12 g/dL",b:"10 g/dL",c:"8 g/dL",d:"7 g/dL",ans:"C",e:"The NICE and AABB guidelines recommend a restrictive transfusion threshold of 7 g/dL for most patients, but 8 g/dL for patients with pre-existing cardiovascular disease or symptomatic anaemia - where reduced O2 delivery is less tolerated.<br><br>Take Notes: Restrictive strategy (7 g/dL) = better or equivalent outcomes in most settings | Liberal strategy (10 g/dL) → historically used, now NOT recommended | Preoperative anaemia → optimise (IV iron) before elective surgery | Cell salvage → reduces allogeneic transfusion in major surgery",t:180},
  {q:"A 70-year-old patient is brought to the emergency department with acute abdomen. His pre-operative risk assessment shows Goldman cardiac risk class III. This indicates:",a:"No cardiac risk",b:"Minimal cardiac risk (<1%)",c:"Moderate cardiac risk (3-10%)",d:"Life-threatening cardiac risk (>25% chance of cardiac complication)",ans:"C",e:"Goldman's Cardiac Risk Index class III (13-25 points) indicates a 3-10% risk of life-threatening cardiac complication or cardiac death - these patients require optimisation and careful intra-operative monitoring before emergency surgery.<br><br>Take Notes: Goldman Class I (0-5 pts) → <1% | Class II (6-12) → 5% | Class III (13-25) → 11% | Class IV (>26) → 78% | Revised Cardiac Risk Index (Lee) → simpler, more widely used now (ischaemic heart disease, CHF, CVD, DM, CKD, high-risk surgery)",t:180},
  {q:"The Caprini score is used perioperatively to:",a:"Assess cardiac risk before major surgery",b:"Stratify venous thromboembolism (VTE) risk and guide prophylaxis type and duration",c:"Predict anastomotic leak after bowel surgery",d:"Grade severity of acute pancreatitis",ans:"B",e:"The Caprini score stratifies VTE risk by summing risk factors (age, type of surgery, mobility, prior DVT, thrombophilia, etc.) - guiding decisions on sequential compression devices alone versus pharmacological prophylaxis (LMWH) versus extended prophylaxis.<br><br>Take Notes: High-risk Caprini (≥5) → LMWH + mechanical prophylaxis + consider 28-day extended LMWH post major cancer surgery | Low-risk → early ambulation + TED stockings | Contraindications to LMWH → active bleeding, neurosurgery, thrombocytopenia",t:180},
  {q:"The concentration of sodium in normal saline (0.9% NaCl) is approximately:",a:"77 mmol/L",b:"130 mmol/L",c:"154 mmol/L",d:"140 mmol/L",ans:"C",e:"0.9% NaCl contains 154 mmol/L of both sodium and chloride - this is higher than plasma chloride (103 mmol/L), so large volumes cause hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis. Hartmann's solution better matches plasma electrolyte composition.<br><br>Take Notes: Normal saline → 154 mmol/L Na+ and Cl- | Hartmann's (Ringer's lactate) → Na+ 131, K+ 5, Ca2+ 2, Cl- 111, lactate 29 mmol/L | Large normal saline volumes → hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis | 5% dextrose → distributes to all body compartments (poor volume expander)",t:180},
  {q:"A patient involved in a high-speed RTA presents with BP 80/50 mmHg, HR 130/min, and distended abdomen. After 2 litres of crystalloid his pressure rises to 90/60 mmHg but then falls again. The most appropriate next intervention is:",a:"Repeat CT scan of abdomen",b:"Continue fluid resuscitation with 2 more litres of saline",c:"Immediate laparotomy (damage control surgery)",d:"Transfuse 2 units packed red cells and observe for 2 hours",ans:"C",e:"Transient response to initial fluid resuscitation followed by relapse indicates ongoing haemorrhage requiring operative haemorrhage control - a 'damage control' laparotomy to control bleeding and contamination before definitive repair when physiology is restored.<br><br>Take Notes: ATLS response classification → responder (haemorrhage controlled), transient responder (ongoing haemorrhage → needs surgery), non-responder (massive haemorrhage → immediate surgery) | Damage control philosophy → haemostasis + contamination control → ICU → definitive repair at 48-72h",t:180},
  {q:"Which of the following is an indication for emergency thoracotomy in the Emergency Department following penetrating chest trauma?",a:"Haemothorax >1500 mL on CXR",b:"Cardiac tamponade on FAST scan in a haemodynamically stable patient",c:"Witnessed cardiac arrest from penetrating trauma with <10 minutes CPR",d:"Tension pneumothorax not responding to needle decompression",ans:"C",e:"Emergency department thoracotomy (EDT) is most successful in penetrating cardiac trauma with witnessed cardiac arrest and <10 minutes of CPR - survival rates of 15-35% have been reported in this select group; blunt trauma EDT has <1% survival.<br><br>Take Notes: EDT indications → penetrating trauma + witnessed arrest + <10 min CPR (best results in stab wounds to heart) | Contraindications → blunt trauma arrest, CPR >10 min, no signs of life | Aims → release cardiac tamponade + control bleeding + open cardiac massage + clamp aorta | FAST → guides tamponade diagnosis",t:180},
  {q:"The most appropriate initial management of a tension pneumothorax in a haemodynamically unstable patient is:",a:"Chest X-ray confirmation before any intervention",b:"CT thorax for accurate assessment",c:"Immediate needle decompression (2nd intercostal space, mid-clavicular line) followed by chest drain",d:"Observe and reassess after oxygen therapy",ans:"C",e:"Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis - immediate needle decompression in the 2nd ICS MCL (or 4th-5th ICS AAL) is performed without waiting for imaging, followed by definitive large-bore chest drain insertion.<br><br>Take Notes: Clinical signs → absent breath sounds, tracheal deviation AWAY from affected side, hypotension, distended neck veins, respiratory distress | A clinical diagnosis - do NOT wait for CXR | Needle decompression → temporary; definitive = chest drain | 4th-5th ICS AAL → now preferred site (less risk of hitting subclavian vessels)",t:180},
  {q:"A patient with blunt abdominal trauma has a positive FAST scan (fluid in Morison's pouch) and is haemodynamically unstable. The next step is:",a:"CT scan of abdomen and pelvis with contrast",b:"Diagnostic peritoneal lavage",c:"Immediate operative exploration (laparotomy)",d:"Repeat FAST after 30 minutes",ans:"C",e:"Haemodynamically unstable patient with positive FAST = intra-abdominal haemorrhage requiring immediate operative haemorrhage control. CT is only appropriate in haemodynamically stable patients - in an unstable patient CT delays life-saving surgery.<br><br>Take Notes: FAST (Focused Assessment with Sonography in Trauma) → screens for pericardial + peritoneal fluid | Stable + positive FAST → CT to characterise injury | Unstable + positive FAST → operating room | DPL → now rarely used, replaced by FAST | Extended FAST (eFAST) → includes thoracic windows for haemothorax/pneumothorax",t:180},
  {q:"Damage control resuscitation in massive haemorrhage follows which ratio of blood components?",a:"Packed red cells: FFP: platelets = 1:0:0",b:"Packed red cells: FFP: platelets = 6:1:1",c:"Packed red cells: FFP: platelets = 1:1:1",d:"FFP alone until cross-matched blood is available",ans:"C",e:"Damage control resuscitation aims to deliver balanced haemostatic resuscitation (red cells + FFP + platelets in 1:1:1 ratio, approximating whole blood) early in massive haemorrhage, avoiding dilutional and consumptive coagulopathy.<br><br>Take Notes: 1:1:1 evidence → PROPPR trial (2015) showed improved haemostasis and survival | Avoid crystalloid-heavy resuscitation (worsens coagulopathy, acidosis, hypothermia = lethal triad) | TXA → tranexamic acid within 3 hours of injury (CRASH-2 trial) | Whole blood → resurging as alternative | Target Hb 70-100 g/L, fibrinogen >1.5 g/L",t:180},
  {q:"Splenic injury grade IV on CT (involving more than 75% of parenchyma or involving hilar vessels) in a haemodynamically STABLE adult patient is best managed by:",a:"Immediate splenectomy",b:"Conservative management with bed rest and serial CT scans",c:"Splenic artery embolisation (SAE)",d:"Observation without imaging follow-up",ans:"C",e:"Splenic artery embolisation has become the intervention of choice for high-grade (III-V) splenic injuries in haemodynamically stable adults - it reduces failure rate of non-operative management from ~25% to ~5% and preserves splenic immune function.<br><br>Take Notes: Non-operative management failure → haemodynamic instability = emergent splenectomy | Grade I-II → observe (NOM success >95%) | Grade III → selective SAE | Grade IV-V + stable → proximal SAE or distal + proximal | Post-splenectomy → vaccines for encapsulated organisms (pneumococcus, meningococcus, Hib)",t:180},
  {q:"In a polytrauma patient with suspected cervical spine injury, which radiological investigation is now the gold standard for initial assessment?",a:"Plain X-rays (AP, lateral, and odontoid peg views)",b:"MRI spine",c:"CT scan from occiput to T1",d:"Flexion-extension X-rays",ans:"C",e:"CT cervical spine (occiput to T1) is the gold standard for initial bony cervical spine assessment in high-energy trauma - it is faster, more sensitive for fractures (90-99% vs 52-54% for plain films), and directly visualises bony anatomy.<br><br>Take Notes: CT → gold standard for bony injury | MRI → best for ligamentous injury, cord injury, soft tissue | Plain films → still used in low-risk mechanism, adequate films required | NEXUS criteria and Canadian C-spine Rule → guide clinical clearance without imaging | C-spine clearance → CT + clinical +/- MRI",t:180},
  {q:"The Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) components and their maximum scores are:",a:"Eyes 4, Verbal 5, Motor 6 (maximum 15)",b:"Eyes 5, Verbal 4, Motor 6 (maximum 15)",c:"Eyes 4, Verbal 5, Motor 5 (maximum 14)",d:"Eyes 3, Verbal 5, Motor 7 (maximum 15)",ans:"A",e:"GCS = Eyes (1-4) + Verbal (1-5) + Motor (1-6), maximum 15, minimum 3 (deep coma). GCS ≤8 = severe TBI requiring airway protection; GCS 9-12 = moderate; 13-15 = mild.<br><br>Take Notes: E4 → spontaneous | E3 → to voice | E2 → to pain | E1 → none | V5 → orientated | V4 → confused | V3 → words | V2 → sounds | V1 → none | M6 → obeys | M5 → localises | M4 → withdraws | M3 → flexion | M2 → extension | M1 → none",t:180},
  {q:"A motorcyclist sustains a high-energy pelvic ring fracture with haemodynamic instability despite 2L crystalloid. Pelvis is bound with a pelvic binder. The next MOST appropriate intervention is:",a:"CT angiography of the pelvis",b:"Transfer to a major trauma centre with haemostasis team → pelvic packing or angioembolisation",c:"External fixator application in ED",d:"Additional 2L normal saline",ans:"B",e:"Haemodynamically unstable pelvic fractures require immediate haemorrhage control - pre-peritoneal pelvic packing is increasingly preferred as it is faster than angioembolisation for venous pelvic bleeding, with angioembolisation reserved for arterial haemorrhage identified on CT in a transiently stable patient.<br><br>Take Notes: Pelvic binder → closes pelvic ring, reduces volume, tamponades venous bleeding | Venous bleeding (85%) → pelvic packing | Arterial bleeding (15%) → angioembolisation | External fixator → definitive pelvic stabilisation after haemorrhage control | Avoid internal fixation acutely (contamination, haemorrhage risk)",t:180},
  {q:"Which of the following rib fractures is associated with the highest risk of pneumonia and mortality in elderly patients?",a:"Single isolated rib fracture",b:"3 or more rib fractures",c:"Bilateral first rib fractures",d:"Floating (isolated) 12th rib fracture",ans:"B",e:"Three or more rib fractures in elderly patients (particularly >65 years) correlate with dramatically increased pneumonia rates and mortality - each additional rib fracture increases pneumonia risk by 27% and mortality by 19% in elderly patients (Eastern multicentre study).<br><br>Take Notes: Flail chest → ≥3 consecutive ribs fractured in ≥2 places (paradoxical breathing) | Elderly rib fractures → admit all for multimodal analgesia, physiotherapy, early consideration of thoracic epidural | Rib fractures → most common cause of morbidity in blunt chest trauma | Haemothorax → drain if >300-500 mL",t:180},
  {q:"Abdominal compartment syndrome is defined as intra-abdominal pressure (IAP) greater than:",a:"10 mmHg with new organ dysfunction",b:"20 mmHg with new or progressive organ dysfunction",c:"15 mmHg with abdominal distension",d:"25 mmHg regardless of clinical signs",ans:"B",e:"Abdominal compartment syndrome (ACS) = IAP >20 mmHg + new/progressive organ dysfunction (renal, cardiovascular, pulmonary, or hepatic). Measured via urinary bladder pressure with patient supine and 25 mL saline instilled.<br><br>Take Notes: Normal IAP → 0-5 mmHg | Intra-abdominal hypertension → IAP ≥12 mmHg | ACS → IAP >20 + organ dysfunction | Treatment → decompressive laparotomy with temporary abdominal closure (Bogota bag, vacuum-assisted) | Risk factors → massive resuscitation, packing, bowel oedema",t:180},
  {q:"In the ATLS primary survey, the 'E' in ABCDE stands for:",a:"Electrocardiogram",b:"Extremities",c:"Exposure and environment control (prevent hypothermia)",d:"Evidence-based assessment",ans:"C",e:"In ATLS, 'E' = Exposure (fully undress the patient to identify all injuries) + Environment control (prevent hypothermia, a component of the lethal triad of trauma).<br><br>Take Notes: ABCDE → Airway (cervical spine), Breathing, Circulation, Disability (neuro), Exposure/Environment | Lethal triad → hypothermia + acidosis + coagulopathy | Log-roll → examine back during primary survey | Rectal temperature → important to identify hypothermia | <35°C → active warming measures",t:180},
  {q:"A 55-year-old man presents with 6 hours of periumbilical pain that has migrated to the right iliac fossa, anorexia, and fever of 38.5°C. WBC 15×10⁹/L. Alvarado score calculation gives 8. The most appropriate management is:",a:"CT abdomen for confirmation before any intervention",b:"Laparoscopic appendicectomy",c:"Observation for 24 hours with antibiotics",d:"Ultrasound of the abdomen only",ans:"B",e:"Alvarado score 8-10 = high probability of acute appendicitis; laparoscopic appendicectomy is indicated. CT is not routinely needed when clinical and scoring criteria are clearly positive - it adds radiation, cost, and delay in straightforward presentations.<br><br>Take Notes: Alvarado score → migration of pain (1), anorexia (1), nausea/vomiting (1), RIF tenderness (2), rebound (1), elevated temperature (1), leukocytosis (2), shift to left (1) | Maximum 10 points | Score ≥7 → operate | 5-6 → observe or CT | <5 → discharge unlikely appendicitis",t:180},
  {q:"A 28-year-old woman presents with right iliac fossa pain and a positive pregnancy test (βhCG 2500 IU/L). Transvaginal ultrasound shows no intrauterine pregnancy and a right adnexal mass. She is haemodynamically stable. The most appropriate management is:",a:"Repeat βhCG in 48 hours only",b:"Immediate salpingectomy",c:"Methotrexate injection if criteria are met (stable, unruptured, ≤3.5 cm, βhCG <1500, no foetal cardiac activity)",d:"Conservative management with serial βhCG monitoring alone",ans:"C",e:"Medical management with methotrexate is appropriate for haemodynamically stable ectopic pregnancy meeting criteria: unruptured, no foetal cardiac activity, βhCG <5000 IU/L (some criteria use <1500), mass ≤3.5 cm, no significant free fluid.<br><br>Take Notes: βhCG >5000 or rising rapidly → higher methotrexate failure rate | Surgical → salpingectomy (preferred over salpingostomy to reduce recurrence) | Ectopic risk factors → PID, prior ectopic, tubal surgery, IUCD, IVF | Most common site → ampullary portion of fallopian tube",t:180},
  {q:"Which finding on CT is the most specific sign of acute appendicitis?",a:"Free fluid in pelvis",b:"Wall thickening of caecum",c:"Dilated appendix >6 mm with periappendiceal fat stranding",d:"Adenopathy in the right iliac fossa",ans:"C",e:"CT appendix diameter >6 mm combined with wall thickening, mucosal enhancement, and periappendiceal fat stranding is the most specific CT finding for acute appendicitis (sensitivity 94%, specificity 95%).<br><br>Take Notes: Appendicolith (faecolith) → increases diagnostic confidence if present | Free perforation → free air, fluid collections, diffuse fat stranding | Phlegmon or abscess → may change management (conservative initially) | Ultrasound → first-line in children and pregnant women (avoids radiation)",t:180},
  {q:"The operation of choice for a perforated peptic ulcer with generalised peritonitis and haemodynamically stable patient is:",a:"Total gastrectomy",b:"Omental patch repair (Graham patch) + thorough peritoneal lavage",c:"Proximal vagotomy and pyloroplasty",d:"Highly selective vagotomy",ans:"B",e:"Graham patch (omental patch repair) is the standard emergency procedure for perforated peptic ulcer - it plugs the perforation with viable omentum, is technically straightforward, and definitive ulcer surgery is deferred as H. pylori eradication treats the underlying cause.<br><br>Take Notes: H. pylori eradication → test and treat after recovery (definitive treatment of peptic ulcer disease) | Laparoscopic repair → equivalent outcomes to open if expertise available | PPI → high-dose IV perioperatively | Mortality → strongly correlated with delay to surgery, age, and comorbidities | Proximal vagotomy → no longer routine (H. pylori era changed management)",t:180},
  {q:"A patient with obstructing sigmoid cancer undergoes an emergency Hartmann procedure. This involves:",a:"Right hemicolectomy with primary anastomosis",b:"Sigmoid colectomy + end colostomy + closure of rectal stump (no anastomosis)",c:"Total colectomy with ileorectal anastomosis",d:"Defunctioning loop colostomy alone without resection",ans:"B",e:"Hartmann's procedure resects the sigmoid colon creating an end colostomy and closes the rectal stump - it avoids a high-risk anastomosis in an unprepared, potentially obstructed or contaminated colon. Colostomy reversal is performed electively 3-6 months later.<br><br>Take Notes: Reversal rate → 50-70% (many patients elderly/unfit for reversal) | Stoma complications → prolapse, retraction, parastomal hernia, skin excoriation | Alternative → primary anastomosis with on-table lavage if patient fit | Subtotal colectomy + end ileostomy → for synchronous proximal colonic obstruction",t:180},
  {q:"A 60-year-old man with known alcoholic cirrhosis develops sudden worsening of ascites, fever, and diffuse abdominal tenderness with rebound. Ascitic tap shows WBC 750 cells/μL with 85% PMNs. The diagnosis and treatment are:",a:"Hepatic encephalopathy; lactulose",b:"Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis; cefotaxime IV + albumin 1.5g/kg",c:"Tuberculous peritonitis; four-drug anti-TB therapy",d:"Secondary peritonitis; emergency laparotomy",ans:"B",e:"SBP is diagnosed by ascitic polymorphonuclear cell count >250 cells/μL without an intra-abdominal surgical source; treatment is IV cefotaxime (or ceftriaxone) with albumin infusion (prevents hepatorenal syndrome in high-risk patients: bilirubin >68 μmol/L or creatinine >88 μmol/L).<br><br>Take Notes: SBP diagnosis → PMN ≥250/μL in ascitic fluid (culture may be negative in 40%) | Most common organisms → E. coli, Klebsiella, streptococci | Albumin → 1.5 g/kg day 1 + 1 g/kg day 3 (reduces HRS) | Prophylaxis → norfloxacin for secondary prophylaxis | SBP + renal failure = poor prognosis (liver transplant consideration)",t:180},
  {q:"The Ranson criteria for predicting severity of acute pancreatitis include all EXCEPT:",a:"Age >55 years",b:"WBC >16,000/μL at admission",c:"Serum calcium <2 mmol/L within 48 hours",d:"Elevated lipase >3× upper limit of normal",ans:"D",e:"Ranson criteria are specific admission criteria (age, WBC, glucose, LDH, AST) and 48-hour criteria (haematocrit fall, BUN rise, calcium, base deficit, fluid sequestration, PaO2) - elevated lipase is not part of the scoring system (it is used for diagnosis, not severity).<br><br>Take Notes: Ranson criteria → 5 admission + 6 at 48h = 11 total | ≥3 criteria = severe pancreatitis | Alternative scores → APACHE II, BISAP, CT severity index (Balthazar) | CT indicated → if diagnosis uncertain or if clinically severe at 48-72h | Most common cause → gallstones + alcohol",t:180},
  {q:"A 72-year-old woman is found to have a 6.5 cm infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm. She has no symptoms. Her operative risk is moderate. The most appropriate management is:",a:"Urgent endovascular stent graft (EVAR) within 24 hours",b:"Elective repair (EVAR preferred if anatomy suitable, otherwise open repair)",c:"Surveillance ultrasound every 3 months",d:"Medical management with beta-blockers only",ans:"B",e:"AAA ≥5.5 cm in men or ≥5.0 cm in women with acceptable operative risk warrants elective repair to prevent rupture (annual rupture risk ~25% for 6.5 cm). EVAR is preferred over open repair if anatomy is suitable (reduced perioperative mortality).<br><br>Take Notes: Rupture risk → <5.5 cm (<2%), 5.5-6 cm (10-20%), >7 cm (>30-50%) per year | EVAR → requires suitable iliac access and proximal neck anatomy | Open repair → higher perioperative mortality but more durable long-term | Surveillance → 4.0-5.4 cm at 3-monthly intervals (UK Vascular Society guidelines)",t:180},
  {q:"A patient presents with acute large bowel obstruction. CT shows a sigmoid volvulus with the 'coffee bean sign'. The FIRST-LINE treatment is:",a:"Emergency resection and colostomy (Hartmann's procedure)",b:"Colonoscopic or flexible sigmoidoscopic decompression",c:"IV fluids and conservative management",d:"Barium enema",ans:"B",e:"Sigmoid volvulus without peritoneal signs is first managed by endoscopic decompression (flexible sigmoidoscopy or rigid sigmoidoscopy with flatus tube placement) - success rate 70-90%. Surgical resection is indicated for failed decompression, ischaemia, perforation, or gangrenous bowel.<br><br>Take Notes: Coffee bean sign (omega loop) → pathognomonic of sigmoid volvulus on plain AXR/CT | Recurrence rate after decompression → 40-90% → elective sigmoid colectomy after bowel prep | Caecal volvulus → requires surgery (colonoscopy rarely successful) | Signs of ischaemia/peritonitis → emergency surgery regardless",t:180},
  {q:"Meckel's diverticulum arises from the persistence of the:",a:"Urachus",b:"Vitelline (omphalomesenteric) duct",c:"Allantois",d:"Hepatic diverticulum",ans:"B",e:"Meckel's diverticulum is the most common congenital anomaly of the GI tract (2% of population) - it results from failure of obliteration of the vitelline duct, occurring on the antimesenteric border of the ileum 2 feet from the ileocaecal valve.<br><br>Take Notes: Rule of 2s → 2% population, 2 inches long, 2 feet from ileocaecal valve, 2:1 male:female, presents before age 2 (in children), 2 types of ectopic mucosa (gastric and pancreatic) | Ectopic gastric mucosa → acid secretion → ileal ulceration → bleeding | Technetium-99m scan → detects ectopic gastric mucosa",t:180},
  {q:"The most common site of gastrointestinal stromal tumour (GIST) is:",a:"Oesophagus",b:"Small intestine",c:"Stomach",d:"Rectum",ans:"C",e:"GISTs most commonly arise in the stomach (60-70%), followed by small intestine (20-25%), colorectum, and oesophagus - they arise from the interstitial cells of Cajal (pacemaker cells of GI tract) and are driven by c-KIT or PDGFRA mutations.<br><br>Take Notes: c-KIT mutation → ~85% of GISTs | CD117 (c-KIT) positive on IHC → diagnostic | Imatinib → neoadjuvant for resectable (downsizing) + adjuvant + unresectable/metastatic | Size + mitotic rate → predict malignant potential | Never biopsied percutaneously (risk of seeding) → surgical excision is diagnostic and therapeutic",t:180},
  {q:"A 45-year-old woman has a 4 cm right lobe hepatic lesion on ultrasound. MRI shows a lesion that is hyperintense on T2 with peripheral nodular enhancement filling in centripetally on delayed phases. The most likely diagnosis is:",a:"Hepatocellular carcinoma",b:"Hepatic adenoma",c:"Haemangioma",d:"Colorectal liver metastasis",ans:"C",e:"Haemangioma has a characteristic MRI pattern - T2 hyperintensity (light bulb sign) with centripetal nodular enhancement filling in on delayed phases mirrors the vascular structure of cavernous haemangiomas (blood pools entering peripherally and filling centripetally).<br><br>Take Notes: HCC (in cirrhosis) → arterial enhancement + venous washout (washout sign) | Hepatic adenoma → homogeneous enhancement, associated with OCP/steroids/glycogen storage disease | Metastases → ring enhancement (peripheral), multiple, hypovascular (CRC) | FNH → central scar with spoke-wheel pattern | Hemangioma → centripetal enhancement (fill in)",t:180},
  {q:"Regarding indirect inguinal hernia, it is classified as indirect because:",a:"It arises medial to the inferior epigastric vessels",b:"It enters the inguinal canal at the deep inguinal ring (lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels) and follows the spermatic cord",c:"It passes through the femoral canal",d:"It arises through Hesselbach's triangle",ans:"B",e:"Indirect inguinal hernias enter the inguinal canal through the deep inguinal ring (lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels), following the path of the processus vaginalis - they are the most common hernia type in all ages and in both sexes.<br><br>Take Notes: Direct inguinal hernia → through Hesselbach's triangle (medial to inferior epigastric vessels), acquired, in older males | Indirect → congenital weakness (patent processus vaginalis), all ages | Femoral hernia → through femoral canal, below and lateral to pubic tubercle, more common in women | Richter hernia → only part of bowel wall in sac (no complete obstruction initially)",t:180},
  {q:"The most serious complication of untreated obstructive jaundice from cholangitis is:",a:"Hyperbilirubinaemia",b:"Vitamin K deficiency and coagulopathy",c:"Ascending cholangitis progressing to septic shock (Charcot's triad → Reynold's pentad)",d:"Gallstone pancreatitis",ans:"C",e:"Charcot's triad (RUQ pain, fever/rigors, jaundice) indicates bacterial cholangitis; Reynold's pentad adds confusion and hypotension - indicating severe ascending cholangitis with bacteraemia, septic shock, and high mortality without urgent biliary decompression.<br><br>Take Notes: Charcot's triad → fever + jaundice + RUQ pain | Reynold's pentad → Charcot's + confusion + hypotension (septic shock) | Urgent ERCP + sphincterotomy + stone extraction → decompresses biliary system | Most common organism → E. coli | Choledocholithiasis → most common cause of ascending cholangitis",t:180},
  {q:"The most common malignant tumour of the thyroid gland is:",a:"Medullary carcinoma",b:"Follicular carcinoma",c:"Papillary carcinoma",d:"Anaplastic carcinoma",ans:"C",e:"Papillary carcinoma is the most common thyroid malignancy (~80%), characterised by Orphan Annie eye nuclei, nuclear grooves, psammoma bodies, and lymphatic spread - it has an excellent prognosis (>95% 10-year survival).<br><br>Take Notes: Papillary → lymphatic spread, most common, best prognosis | Follicular → haematogenous spread (bone, lung), need vascular invasion for malignancy | Medullary → C cells, calcitonin, MEN2A/2B | Anaplastic → rapidly fatal, worst prognosis | Treatment → total thyroidectomy + radioiodine (except medullary)",t:180},
  {q:"A 45-year-old woman has a 2 cm discrete breast lump that is mobile, non-tender, and firm. Triple assessment shows a firm non-tender lump (clinical), irregular hypoechoic lesion on USS with posterior acoustic shadowing, and core biopsy shows invasive ductal carcinoma grade 2 ER+/PR+/HER2-. The MOST appropriate primary surgical option is:",a:"Wide local excision (WLE) + sentinel lymph node biopsy",b:"Mastectomy alone",c:"Chemotherapy first then surgery",d:"Lumpectomy without axillary assessment",ans:"A",e:"For most T2N0 breast cancers that meet criteria, breast-conserving surgery (WLE) plus sentinel lymph node biopsy is the standard first-line surgical approach - equivalent oncological outcomes to mastectomy with better quality of life.<br><br>Take Notes: WLE criteria → adequate tumour:breast ratio, clear margins achievable, acceptable cosmesis | Sentinel LNB → standard axillary assessment for clinically node-negative | Mastectomy → if WLE not possible (large tumour, multifocal, BRCA1/2, patient preference) | ER+ → adjuvant endocrine therapy (tamoxifen or aromatase inhibitor)",t:180},
  {q:"Which of the following is the MOST common presentation of primary hyperparathyroidism?",a:"Symptomatic hypercalcaemia with renal stones, bone disease, and psychiatric symptoms",b:"Asymptomatic hypercalcaemia detected incidentally on routine blood tests",c:"Acute hypercalcaemic crisis",d:"Pathological fractures as initial presentation",ans:"B",e:"The majority of primary hyperparathyroidism (~80%) is now detected as asymptomatic hypercalcaemia on routine biochemistry - classic 'bones, stones, groans, and psychic moans' presentation is now uncommon in the developed world.<br><br>Take Notes: Primary HPT → elevated PTH + elevated calcium | Most common cause → solitary parathyroid adenoma (80-85%) | Localisation → Sestamibi scan + USS (first-line) | Indications for surgery in asymptomatic → age <50, Ca >2.85 mmol/L, T-score <-2.5, CrCl <60 mL/min, 24-h urinary calcium >400 mg | Multigland disease → 4-gland exploration",t:180},
  {q:"A patient with MEN2B syndrome (RET proto-oncogene mutation) will most likely develop:",a:"Parathyroid adenoma + pituitary adenoma + pancreatic tumour",b:"Medullary thyroid carcinoma + phaeochromocytoma + mucosal neuromas (marfanoid habitus)",c:"Parathyroid hyperplasia + medullary thyroid carcinoma + phaeochromocytoma",d:"Insulinoma + glucagonoma + VIPoma",ans:"B",e:"MEN2B includes medullary thyroid carcinoma (>95%), phaeochromocytoma (50%), and mucosal neuromas (lips, tongue, GI tract) with marfanoid habitus and no parathyroid disease - the most aggressive MEN2 variant, prophylactic thyroidectomy in first 6 months of life.<br><br>Take Notes: MEN1 → 3Ps: Pituitary + Parathyroid + Pancreatic (PPoma) | MEN2A → MTC + Phaeochromocytoma + Parathyroid hyperplasia | MEN2B → MTC + Phaeochromocytoma + Mucosal neuromas (no parathyroid) | All MEN syndromes → autosomal dominant | RET mutation → MEN2A and 2B | MEN1 mutation → menin (tumour suppressor)",t:180},
  {q:"The axillary lymph node levels (I, II, III) are defined in relation to which anatomical structure?",a:"Pectoralis major muscle",b:"Pectoralis minor muscle",c:"Axillary vein",d:"Latissimus dorsi muscle",ans:"B",e:"Axillary lymph node levels are defined by their relationship to pectoralis minor: Level I = lateral, Level II = behind (deep to), Level III = medial to pectoralis minor. Level II also includes Rotter's (interpectoral) nodes.<br><br>Take Notes: Level I → lateral to pectoralis minor (low axilla) | Level II → behind pectoralis minor | Level III → medial to pectoralis minor (apical) | Standard ALND → removes levels I and II | Full ALND (level III) → in node-positive disease or Rotter's node involvement",t:180},
  {q:"A 55-year-old male smoker presents with a 3 cm hard fixed node in the upper anterior cervical chain, with no visible primary. CT shows a necrotic node at level II. The most appropriate FIRST investigation is:",a:"Fine needle aspiration cytology (FNAC) of the node",b:"CT of chest, abdomen, and pelvis",c:"PET-CT scan",d:"Panendoscopy under general anaesthesia",ans:"A",e:"FNAC is the first-line investigation for a suspicious cervical lymph node - it is quick, minimally invasive, and guides subsequent management (biopsy of node only if FNAC non-diagnostic, as excision biopsy can compromise subsequent surgery).<br><br>Take Notes: Excision biopsy of cervical node → only if FNAC and core biopsy non-diagnostic | CT neck/chest → identifies primary and guides staging after tissue diagnosis | PET-CT → identifies unknown primary (in 20-30% of CUP after conventional imaging) | Unknown primary head and neck cancer → often HPV-related oropharyngeal primary (base of tongue, tonsil)",t:180},
  {q:"Which investigation is the gold standard for diagnosis of hyperfunctioning parathyroid adenoma before neck exploration?",a:"CT neck",b:"Sestamibi parathyroid scintigraphy (Technetium-99m MIBI scan)",c:"MRI neck",d:"USS neck alone",ans:"B",e:"Sestamibi MIBI scan combined with SPECT (or 4D CT) is the gold standard functional localisation study for parathyroid adenoma - it identifies ~75-85% of single adenomas and guides minimally invasive parathyroidectomy (avoiding 4-gland exploration).<br><br>Take Notes: Sestamibi → taken up by mitochondria-rich parathyroid adenoma | Combined with USS → concordance guides focused (minimally invasive) parathyroidectomy | Intraoperative PTH monitoring → confirms complete resection (PTH falls >50% in 10 min) | 4D CT → best for multigland disease or reoperative neck | Multigland disease → bilateral exploration",t:180},
  {q:"A 35-year-old woman presents with a solitary thyroid nodule measuring 2.5 cm. Fine needle aspiration cytology (FNAC) reports Thy3f (follicular neoplasm, atypia). The next step in management is:",a:"Thyroid ultrasound surveillance only",b:"Hemithyroidectomy (diagnostic lobectomy)",c:"Total thyroidectomy immediately",d:"Radioiodine ablation",ans:"B",e:"Thy3f (follicular neoplasm) cannot be distinguished as benign adenoma vs follicular carcinoma on cytology (capsular and vascular invasion only visible on histology) - hemithyroidectomy provides definitive histological diagnosis; completion thyroidectomy if malignancy confirmed.<br><br>Take Notes: UK Thy classification → Thy1 (non-diagnostic) → Thy2 (benign) → Thy3a/3f (follicular atypia/neoplasm) → Thy4 (suspicious malignant) → Thy5 (malignant) | Follicular carcinoma diagnosis → requires histology (capsular/vascular invasion) | Bethesda system → US equivalent | Hemithyroidectomy → diagnostic + therapeutic for Thy3f",t:180},
  {q:"A 70-year-old hypertensive male presents with sudden severe back pain radiating to the groin, a pulsatile epigastric mass, and haemodynamic instability. The most appropriate management is:",a:"CT scan of the abdomen with contrast urgently",b:"Immediate transfer to operating theatre for emergency open AAA repair",c:"Endovascular repair (EVAR) as first choice",d:"USS to confirm diagnosis before surgery",ans:"B",e:"Clinical diagnosis of ruptured AAA requires immediate surgery without waiting for imaging in a haemodynamically unstable patient - CT is only appropriate if the diagnosis is uncertain AND the patient is stable enough to survive the delay.<br><br>Take Notes: Classic triad → sudden severe back/flank pain + pulsatile abdominal mass + haemodynamic shock | Differential → renal colic, MI, mesenteric ischaemia | EVAR in ruptured AAA → increasingly used in centres with 24-hour EVAR capability | Permissive hypotension → target SBP 50-70 mmHg until aortic control | Mortality → 40-50% with surgery, ~80-90% without",t:180},
  {q:"The ankle-brachial pressure index (ABPI) value indicating critical limb ischaemia is:",a:"<0.9",b:"<0.5",c:"<0.3",d:">1.3 (non-compressible vessels)",ans:"C",e:"ABPI <0.5 indicates severe ischaemia; ABPI <0.3 (or toe pressure <30 mmHg) indicates critical limb ischaemia (CLI) with rest pain, ulceration, or gangrene - limb loss is imminent without revascularisation.<br><br>Take Notes: Normal ABPI → 0.9-1.2 | 0.7-0.9 → mild PAD | 0.5-0.7 → moderate | <0.5 → severe | <0.3 → critical | >1.3 → non-compressible (calcified, diabetic) → use toe pressure or duplex | CLI → Fontaine stage III (rest pain) + IV (tissue loss)",t:180},
  {q:"A 65-year-old patient develops acute limb ischaemia 6 hours after onset. The limb is cold, pale, pulseless, paraesthetic, and painful but not yet paralysed (no motor deficit). The most appropriate initial management is:",a:"IV heparin and immediate surgical embolectomy",b:"Catheter-directed thrombolysis",c:"IV heparin + urgent surgical embolectomy (within 4-6 hours) for embolic occlusion",d:"Amputation as limb is non-salvageable",ans:"C",e:"Acute limb ischaemia with pain, pallor, pulselessness, and paraesthesia but intact motor function is a surgical emergency with viable limb - immediate heparinisation prevents clot propagation and urgent embolectomy (within 4-6 hours) restores flow before irreversible muscle necrosis occurs.<br><br>Take Notes: 6Ps → Pain, Pallor, Pulselessness, Paraesthesia, Paralysis, Perishingly cold | Motor deficit + fixed staining → irreversible ischaemia → amputation | Embolus → sudden onset, no prior claudication, AF (atrial fibrillation), limb feels soft | Thrombosis → on background of PAD, gradual, calcified vessels on imaging | CDT → for thrombosis in non-viable limb",t:180},
  {q:"A 75-year-old woman falls on her outstretched hand and sustains a Colles fracture. Classic radiological features include:",a:"Dorsal angulation, dorsal displacement, radial shortening, and radial deviation (dinner fork deformity)",b:"Volar angulation and volar displacement (Smith's fracture)",c:"Intra-articular fracture with dorsal comminution only",d:"Isolated ulnar styloid fracture",ans:"A",e:"Colles fracture (distal radius within 2.5 cm of articular surface) classically shows dorsal angulation (apex volar), dorsal displacement, radial shortening, radial tilt, and supination of the distal fragment - producing the dinner fork/bayonet deformity.<br><br>Take Notes: Colles → FOOSH in elderly osteoporotic, dorsal angulation | Smith's (reverse Colles) → fall on dorsum of flexed wrist → volar angulation | Barton → intra-articular shear fracture (dorsal or volar) | Complication → acute carpal tunnel syndrome, EPL rupture, malunion",t:180},
  {q:"Avascular necrosis of the femoral head most commonly follows fracture of:",a:"Femoral shaft",b:"Femoral neck (intracapsular)",c:"Intertrochanteric fracture",d:"Subtrochanteric fracture",ans:"B",e:"Intracapsular femoral neck fractures disrupt the retinacular (superior) vessels - branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery - that supply the femoral head, causing AVN in 15-30% of displaced fractures.<br><br>Take Notes: Blood supply to femoral head → mainly medial circumflex femoral artery (via retinacular vessels) | Garden classification → I (incomplete), II (complete undisplaced), III (partial displacement), IV (complete displacement) | Garden III/IV → high AVN risk → hemiarthroplasty in elderly | Young patients → urgent ORIF to preserve femoral head",t:180},
  {q:"The most sensitive investigation for early detection of osteomyelitis in the first 48 hours when plain X-ray is normal is:",a:"CT scan of the affected region",b:"MRI of the affected bone",c:"Technetium bone scan",d:"Ultrasound",ans:"B",e:"MRI is the most sensitive early imaging modality for osteomyelitis - it detects bone marrow oedema within 1-3 days of onset when plain X-rays are normal (radiological changes only visible at 10-14 days when >30-50% of bone mineral is lost).<br><br>Take Notes: Plain X-ray → changes at 10-14 days (periosteal reaction, lytic lesion) | MRI → most sensitive early (T2 hyperintensity in marrow) | Bone scan → sensitive but less specific than MRI; useful when MRI unavailable | USS → identifies subperiosteal abscess (guides aspiration in children) | Gold standard culture → bone biopsy (microbiological confirmation)",t:180},
  {q:"A 14-year-old boy presents with a 3-month history of progressive right knee pain worse at night, dramatically relieved by aspirin, with a CT showing a small nidus of <1 cm surrounded by sclerotic bone in the distal femoral metaphysis. The diagnosis is:",a:"Osteosarcoma",b:"Ewing's sarcoma",c:"Osteoid osteoma",d:"Chondroblastoma",ans:"C",e:"Osteoid osteoma - nocturnal pain relieved by NSAIDs (particularly aspirin), a small lucent nidus (<1 cm) surrounded by reactive sclerosis on CT - is pathognomonic. Treatment is radiofrequency ablation (most effective and least invasive).<br><br>Take Notes: Osteoid osteoma → nocturnal pain relieved by aspirin (prostaglandin-mediated) | Age 10-30 years, males | Nidus → <1 cm, central lucent | Treatment → RFA (radiofrequency ablation) is first-line | Osteoblastoma → similar but nidus >1 cm, vertebrae common, pain less reliably relieved by aspirin",t:180},
  {q:"The segond fracture is a small avulsion fracture of the lateral tibial condyle and indicates injury to which structure?",a:"Medial collateral ligament",b:"Anterior cruciate ligament (indirect sign)",c:"Posterior cruciate ligament",d:"Lateral collateral ligament directly",ans:"B",e:"The Segond fracture is an avulsion of the anterolateral ligament (ALL) from the lateral tibial condyle - it is a reliable indirect sign of ACL rupture (occurs in 75-100% of cases with Segond fracture) and is a high-specificity radiological marker for ACL injury.<br><br>Take Notes: Segond fracture → lateral tibial rim avulsion | Highly specific for ACL injury | Also associated with → meniscal tears, posterolateral corner injury | Reverse Segond → medial tibial avulsion → associated with PCL injury | Segond named after Paul Segond (French surgeon, 1879)",t:180},
  {q:"Which classification system is used for open fractures and guides antibiotic prophylaxis and soft tissue management?",a:"Garden classification",b:"Gustilo-Anderson classification",c:"Danis-Weber classification",d:"AO classification",ans:"B",e:"Gustilo-Anderson classifies open fractures I-III (IIIA, IIIB, IIIC) based on wound size, degree of contamination, periosteal stripping, and vascular status - it directly guides antibiotic regimen and need for plastic surgery involvement (IIIB/IIIC).<br><br>Take Notes: Type I → wound <1 cm, clean | Type II → 1-10 cm, moderate contamination | Type IIIA → >10 cm, adequate soft tissue cover | Type IIIB → >10 cm, requires flap coverage | Type IIIC → any size + arterial injury requiring repair | Antibiotics → cefazolin ± metronidazole ± gentamicin depending on type",t:180},
  {q:"Lateral epicondylitis (tennis elbow) results from degeneration at which tendon origin?",a:"Common flexor origin at medial epicondyle",b:"Extensor carpi radialis brevis origin at lateral epicondyle",c:"Biceps tendon at the radial tuberosity",d:"Triceps at the olecranon",ans:"B",e:"Lateral epicondylitis involves angiofibroblastic degeneration (not true inflammation) of the extensor carpi radialis brevis (ECRB) origin at the lateral epicondyle, causing localised tenderness aggravated by resisted wrist extension.<br><br>Take Notes: Lateral epicondylitis → ECRB degeneration | Medial epicondylitis (golfer's elbow) → common flexor origin | Mill's test → pain on passive wrist flexion with extended elbow | Treatment → physiotherapy (eccentric exercises), corticosteroid injection, PRP, rarely surgery | Resisted middle finger extension → specific provocative test for ECRB",t:180},
  {q:"Compartment syndrome pressure threshold for fasciotomy is when compartment pressure is within how many mmHg of diastolic blood pressure?",a:"10 mmHg",b:"20 mmHg",c:"30 mmHg",d:"40 mmHg",ans:"C",e:"Fasciotomy is indicated when compartment pressure rises to within 30 mmHg of diastolic blood pressure (delta pressure <30 mmHg) - this threshold correlates with capillary perfusion pressure and muscle ischaemia onset.<br><br>Take Notes: Normal compartment pressure → 0-8 mmHg | Delta pressure (diastolic - compartment) <30 mmHg → fasciotomy | Clinical signs → pain out of proportion + pain on passive stretch + tense compartment | 4-compartment lower leg fasciotomy → single or double incision technique | Complications of delayed fasciotomy → Volkmann contracture, nerve palsy, renal failure (myoglobinuria)",t:180},
  {q:"Which of the following best describes the radiological appearance of Paget's disease of bone?",a:"Ground glass appearance",b:"Onion skin periosteal reaction",c:"Mixed lytic and sclerotic lesions with cortical thickening and bone enlargement",d:"Soap bubble appearance",ans:"C",e:"Paget's disease shows a mixed lytic-sclerotic pattern with cortical thickening, bone enlargement (particularly skull, pelvis, spine, femur, tibia) - the 'blade of grass' lytic front advances distally in long bones, followed by sclerotic repair.<br><br>Take Notes: Elevated ALP (often markedly) with normal calcium and phosphate | Markedly elevated bone turnover markers | Complications → pathological fracture, high-output cardiac failure, sarcomatous change (<1%), deafness (skull) | Treatment → bisphosphonates (zoledronic acid most potent) | Skull → cotton wool appearance on X-ray",t:180},
  {q:"Salter-Harris type II fracture (most common physeal injury) involves:",a:"The physis only",b:"The physis and epiphysis only",c:"The physis and metaphysis (not the epiphysis)",d:"The physis and both metaphysis and epiphysis",ans:"C",e:"Salter-Harris type II fractures involve the physis (growth plate) and metaphysis - the fracture propagates along the physis and exits through the metaphysis, creating a triangular metaphyseal fragment (Thurston-Holland fragment). Excellent prognosis as epiphysis is spared.<br><br>Take Notes: S1 → through physis only | S2 → physis + metaphysis (most common, ~75%) | S3 → physis + epiphysis | S4 → through all three | S5 → crush/compression of physis (worst prognosis) | Mnemonic SALTER → Slipped, Above, Lower, Through, Rammed (S5) | S5 → may cause growth arrest",t:180},
  {q:"A patient undergoes elective CABG. After cardiopulmonary bypass, the surgeon finds ST-elevation in the inferior leads with haemodynamic compromise. The most likely cause is:",a:"Air embolism to the left anterior descending artery territory",b:"Technical problem with the RCA graft (kinking, air, or thrombosis)",c:"Inadequate cardioplegia",d:"Protamine reaction",ans:"B",e:"Inferior ST elevation post-CPB suggests RCA graft failure - the most common cause of perioperative myocardial ischaemia following CABG. Immediate assessment and graft revision are required before sternal closure.<br><br>Take Notes: Post-bypass ECG → standard monitoring in cardiac surgery ICU | Inferior leads → RCA territory | Anterior leads → LAD territory | Air embolism → right coronary (first filled with air from aorta) → causes RCA territory ischaemia | Treatment → graft revision, intracoronary nitrates, mechanical support (IABP) if needed",t:180},
  {q:"A 68-year-old man undergoes aortic valve replacement for severe aortic stenosis. Post-operatively he has low cardiac output syndrome (LCOS). Which haemodynamic parameter should be targeted FIRST?",a:"Reduce heart rate to <60 bpm",b:"Optimise preload (filling pressures), then contractility, then afterload",c:"Start vasopressors immediately",d:"Commence emergency re-exploration",ans:"B",e:"LCOS management follows a stepwise approach: optimise preload (CVP 8-12, PCWP 12-15 mmHg) first, then improve contractility with inotropes (dobutamine/milrinone), then reduce afterload (vasodilators), and finally consider mechanical support (IABP, VAD).<br><br>Take Notes: LCOS definition → CI <2.2 L/min/m² with signs of organ hypoperfusion | Sequential therapy → preload → contractility → afterload | IABP → reduces afterload + augments diastolic coronary perfusion | Milrinone → PDE3 inhibitor, positive inotropy + vasodilation (useful in RV failure)",t:180},
  {q:"The most common non-traumatic cause of spontaneous pneumothorax in a young tall male patient is:",a:"Tuberculosis",b:"Blebs or bullae rupture at lung apex (primary spontaneous pneumothorax)",c:"Pulmonary fibrosis",d:"COPD emphysematous bullae",ans:"B",e:"Primary spontaneous pneumothorax typically occurs in young, tall, thin males without underlying lung disease - rupture of apical subpleural blebs or bullae releases air into the pleural space. Up to 50% recurrence after first episode.<br><br>Take Notes: First episode → aspiration or observation if small | Recurrence prevention → thoracoscopic pleurodesis (VATS) + bullectomy after 2nd episode or bilateral | Catamenial pneumothorax → in women, associated with menstruation and endometriosis | Secondary spontaneous → underlying lung disease (COPD, CF, Marfan syndrome) | High-resolution CT → identifies blebs",t:180},
  {q:"In cardiac surgery, the term 'aortic cross-clamp time' refers to:",a:"Time to insert the cross clamp on the IVC",b:"Time during which the aorta is clamped and the heart is arrested with cardioplegia (ischaemic time)",c:"Time from sternotomy to CPB initiation",d:"Total bypass time including rewarming",ans:"B",e:"Aortic cross-clamp (ACC) time is the period of cardiac ischaemia - from aorta clamping + cardioplegia delivery to clamp removal and cardiac reperfusion. Prolonged ACC time correlates with myocardial injury and poor postoperative cardiac function.<br><br>Take Notes: Normal ACC time → <90-120 minutes for most procedures | Myocardial protection → cold crystalloid or blood cardioplegia | Warm blood cardioplegia → 'hot shot' at end of ACC reduces reperfusion injury | Total bypass time → includes cooling and rewarming | Off-pump CABG → avoids ACC entirely",t:180},
  {q:"The best predictor of operative mortality for CABG surgery is:",a:"Ejection fraction alone",b:"EuroSCORE II (European System for Cardiac Operative Risk Evaluation)",c:"Number of grafts required",d:"Age alone",ans:"B",e:"EuroSCORE II is the most widely validated preoperative risk stratification tool for cardiac surgery in Europe, incorporating patient-related, cardiac-related, and operation-related risk factors to predict 30-day operative mortality.<br><br>Take Notes: EuroSCORE II → age, gender, creatinine, extracardiac arteriopathy, neurological dysfunction, previous cardiac surgery, LVEF, urgency, critical preoperative state, procedure type | STS score → widely used in North America | Both validated, EuroSCORE II updated 2012 | TAVI vs CABG decisions in aortic stenosis → guided by both scores",t:180},
  {q:"Tetralogy of Fallot comprises:",a:"VSD + pulmonary stenosis + overriding aorta + right ventricular hypertrophy",b:"ASD + pulmonary hypertension + left ventricular hypertrophy + great vessel transposition",c:"VSD + ASD + pulmonary atresia + right aortic arch",d:"PDA + coarctation + VSD + pulmonary stenosis",ans:"A",e:"Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF) consists of four features: large malalignment VSD, subvalvular pulmonary stenosis (RVOTO), overriding aorta (>50% over VSD), and RVH (consequence of the first three). It is the most common cyanotic congenital heart disease beyond infancy.<br><br>Take Notes: Boot-shaped heart (coeur en sabot) on CXR + oligaemic lung fields | Tet spells → hypercyanotic episodes (infundibular spasm) → squatting increases SVR | Total surgical correction → VSD patch + RVOT relief | Definitive repair → typically at 3-6 months | PGE1 → maintains PDA patency in neonatal cyanotic CHD",t:180},
  {q:"Post-pneumonectomy space syndrome is managed by:",a:"Chest drain insertion",b:"Gradual obliteration of the space occurs naturally - no intervention needed in most cases",c:"Immediate re-exploration",d:"Artificial implants or saline injection to fill the space",ans:"B",e:"The post-pneumonectomy space normally fills with fluid and fibrosis over weeks to months - this is physiological and expected. Complications of rapid fluid accumulation (empyema, bronchopleural fistula) rather than the space itself require intervention.<br><br>Take Notes: Post-pneumonectomy space complications → empyema, bronchopleural fistula, post-pneumonectomy pulmonary oedema (contralateral lung) | Bronchopleural fistula → air-fluid level changes, surgical emphysema → Clagett procedure or omental transposition | Space itself → does NOT need filling unless BPF present | Early contralateral mediastinal shift → expected and monitored",t:180},
  {q:"A 35-year-old man presents with sudden-onset 'thunderclap' headache (worst of his life), neck stiffness, and photophobia. CT head is normal. The next step is:",a:"MRI brain with gadolinium",b:"Lumbar puncture (4-6 hours after onset) to detect xanthochromia",c:"Discharge with analgesia and review in 24 hours",d:"CTA head to look for aneurysm immediately",ans:"B",e:"Normal CT in suspected subarachnoid haemorrhage (SAH) requires lumbar puncture 4-6 hours after onset to detect xanthochromia (yellow CSF from oxyhaemoglobin → bilirubin) - CT sensitivity decreases over time (98% at 6h, falling to <50% at 5 days).<br><br>Take Notes: CT → most sensitive within 6h of SAH (NICE guidelines) | LP 4-6h later → xanthochromia (spectrophotometry) if CT negative | CTA → after SAH confirmed to identify aneurysm | Most common cause → berry aneurysm rupture at AComA | Nimodipine → reduces cerebral vasospasm (give for 21 days)",t:180},
  {q:"The Monroe-Kellie doctrine states that:",a:"Intracranial pressure is determined by mean arterial pressure only",b:"The skull is a rigid box - an increase in volume of any intracranial component must be compensated by a decrease in another to maintain constant ICP",c:"CSF production is constant at all ICP levels",d:"ICP fluctuates with cardiac cycle only in pathological states",ans:"B",e:"The Monro-Kellie hypothesis states that the total volume of intracranial contents (brain parenchyma, blood, CSF) is fixed within the rigid skull - any volume increase must be compensated (initially by CSF displacement, then blood) before ICP rises exponentially.<br><br>Take Notes: Intracranial compliance → initially good (CSF displaced to spinal canal) then rapidly deteriorates | Normal ICP → <15 mmHg | Cushing reflex → bradycardia + hypertension + irregular breathing (late sign of raised ICP, brainstem compression) | CPP = MAP - ICP (target >60 mmHg)",t:180},
  {q:"A 25-year-old male sustains a head injury in an RTA. He was briefly unconscious, then completely lucid, then deteriorates rapidly with a dilated fixed right pupil and contralateral hemiparesis 3 hours later. CT shows a biconvex (lenticular) hyperdense collection. The diagnosis is:",a:"Acute subdural haematoma",b:"Cerebral contusion",c:"Extradural (epidural) haematoma",d:"Diffuse axonal injury",ans:"C",e:"Extradural haematoma classically presents with a lucid interval (brief unconsciousness → conscious → rapid deterioration) from arterial bleeding (middle meningeal artery) accumulating between skull and dura, forming a biconvex (lenticular) hyperdense lesion on CT.<br><br>Take Notes: EDH → arterial (middle meningeal artery), temporal bone fracture in 85%, biconvex shape | SDH → venous (bridging veins), crescent-shaped, follows brain surface | CSDH → elderly, minor trauma, develops over weeks | Treatment → urgent craniotomy and evacuation | 'Talk and die' syndrome → classical description of EDH lucid interval",t:180},
  {q:"The most common primary brain tumour in adults is:",a:"Meningioma",b:"Schwannoma",c:"Glioblastoma multiforme (grade IV astrocytoma)",d:"Oligodendroglioma",ans:"C",e:"Glioblastoma multiforme (WHO grade IV glioma) is the most common malignant primary brain tumour in adults (~45% of all gliomas), with a median survival of 14-16 months with optimal treatment (surgery + radiotherapy + temozolomide).<br><br>Take Notes: GBM → ring-enhancing lesion on MRI, crosses corpus callosum (butterfly glioma) | Meningioma → most common benign intracranial tumour | Most common primary brain tumour overall (benign + malignant) → meningioma | Treatment → maximal safe resection + radiotherapy + concurrent/adjuvant temozolomide (Stupp protocol) | MGMT promoter methylation → better response to temozolomide",t:180},
  {q:"Central cord syndrome (the most common incomplete spinal cord injury) is characterised by:",a:"Complete motor and sensory loss below the level of injury",b:"Ipsilateral motor and proprioception loss with contralateral pain/temperature loss",c:"Greater weakness in upper limbs than lower limbs with bladder dysfunction, commonly in elderly with cervical spondylosis following hyperextension",d:"Perianal sensory preservation with loss of voluntary anal sphincter control",ans:"C",e:"Central cord syndrome results from hyperextension injury in a spondylotic cervical spine - central cord damage disproportionately affects the medially located cervical fibres (upper limbs) more than the laterally located lumbar/sacral fibres (lower limbs).<br><br>Take Notes: Central cord → upper > lower limb weakness, bladder dysfunction (urinary retention), sacral sensory preservation | Brown-Séquard → ipsilateral motor loss + contralateral pain/temp loss | Anterior cord → motor + spinothalamic loss, preserved dorsal column | Conus → mixed upper and lower motor neuron signs",t:180},
  {q:"Cauda equina syndrome is an orthopaedic/neurosurgical emergency. The most reliable clinical finding is:",a:"Back pain radiating to both legs",b:"Bladder dysfunction (urinary retention or incontinence) with reduced perineal sensation",c:"Bilateral absence of ankle reflexes",d:"Bilateral leg weakness",ans:"B",e:"Bladder dysfunction (usually urinary retention but can be overflow incontinence) combined with perianal/saddle anaesthesia is the most reliable and clinically defining feature of cauda equina syndrome requiring urgent MRI and surgical decompression.<br><br>Take Notes: CES complete → motor, sensory, and sphincter dysfunction | CES incomplete → partial dysfunction, better prognosis | Red flags → bilateral leg weakness, saddle anaesthesia, bladder/bowel dysfunction | Urgent MRI (within 4 hours) → confirms diagnosis | Decompression within 48h → best urological recovery, surgery outcomes time-sensitive",t:180},
  {q:"The most common cause of non-traumatic subarachnoid haemorrhage is:",a:"Arteriovenous malformation (AVM)",b:"Berry aneurysm rupture",c:"Hypertensive haemorrhage",d:"Cavernous malformation",ans:"B",e:"Berry (saccular) aneurysm rupture accounts for ~85% of non-traumatic SAH. They most commonly arise at branching points in the anterior circulation - anterior communicating artery (most common), posterior communicating artery, and MCA bifurcation.<br><br>Take Notes: AComA → most common berry aneurysm site | PComA aneurysm → CN III palsy (pupil-involving, painful) | AVM → typically presents with haemorrhage in younger patients | Investigation → CT → LP → CTA/DSA | Treatment → surgical clipping or endovascular coiling (ISAT trial → coiling superior in most cases)",t:180},
  {q:"Normal pressure hydrocephalus (NPH) classically presents with the triad of:",a:"Headache + vomiting + papilloedema",b:"Gait disturbance + cognitive decline + urinary incontinence (Hakim-Adams triad)",c:"Seizures + visual changes + diplopia",d:"Neck stiffness + fever + photophobia",ans:"B",e:"NPH (Hakim-Adams syndrome) presents with the classic triad of magnetic gait (broad-based, shuffling, as if feet stuck to floor), cognitive impairment (frontal lobe type), and urinary incontinence - treated by CSF shunting.<br><br>Take Notes: Ventricles dilated out of proportion to cortical atrophy on CT/MRI | CSF tap test → improves gait transiently → predicts shunt response | Treatment → ventriculoperitoneal shunting | Gait impairment responds best to shunting | Prognosis → best if treated early before dementia is severe | Evans ratio >0.3 → suggests ventriculomegaly",t:180},
  {q:"The posterior communicating artery aneurysm compresses CN III causing a painful third nerve palsy. Which pupillary finding distinguishes surgical from medical third nerve palsy?",a:"Bilateral miosis",b:"Ipsilateral mydriasis with complete pupil involvement distinguishes surgical (compressive) palsy",c:"Unaffected pupil (pupil-sparing) distinguishes surgical palsy",d:"Constricted affected pupil",ans:"B",e:"Compressive (surgical) CN III palsy involves the superficially located parasympathetic pupillomotor fibres first, causing ipsilateral fixed dilated pupil - distinguishing it from ischaemic (medical) palsy where pupillomotor fibres are spared (pupil-sparing).<br><br>Take Notes: Pupil-involved (mydriasis) → compressive cause (aneurysm, herniation) → surgical emergency | Pupil-sparing → ischaemic (diabetic) → medical management | PComA aneurysm → painful CN III palsy | Uncal herniation → also causes ipsilateral fixed dilated pupil from CN III compression | Medical CN III palsy → diabetes most common cause",t:180},
  {q:"The MOST appropriate initial management of a stable patient with an acute C5 burst fracture and incomplete spinal cord injury is:",a:"High-dose methylprednisolone infusion (NASCIS protocol)",b:"Urgent surgical decompression and stabilisation",c:"Cervical orthosis alone and physiotherapy",d:"Traction and bed rest for 6 weeks",ans:"B",e:"Incomplete spinal cord injury with compressive C5 burst fracture requires urgent surgical decompression and stabilisation - neurological recovery is time-dependent and decompression within 24 hours improves outcomes. High-dose methylprednisolone is no longer recommended (NASCIS studies discredited).<br><br>Take Notes: High-dose methylprednisolone (NASCIS protocol) → withdrawn from guidelines (modest benefit, significant complications) | Timing of surgery → within 24h for incomplete SCI improves neurological recovery | Complete SCI → surgery for stability, not neurological recovery | Halo vest → external stabilisation for some fractures | ASIA classification → grades A (complete) to E (normal)",t:180},
  {q:"A 60-year-old man with haematuria has a CT urogram showing a 4 cm right renal mass with arterial enhancement and venous phase washout, and AFP is normal. No evidence of metastasis. The most likely diagnosis and management are:",a:"Oncocytoma; observation",b:"Clear cell RCC; partial or radical nephrectomy depending on anatomy",c:"Transitional cell carcinoma; nephroureterectomy",d:"Angiomyolipoma; embolisation",ans:"B",e:"Enhancing renal mass with arterial enhancement and washout in a solid lesion is characteristic of RCC (most commonly clear cell type); for a 4 cm tumour with no metastases, partial nephrectomy (nephron-sparing) is preferred when technically feasible.<br><br>Take Notes: Small renal mass (≤4 cm) → partial nephrectomy preferred (nephron-sparing) | Larger masses or central location → radical nephrectomy | Active surveillance → option for elderly/comorbid | Angiomyolipoma → fat-containing lesion, echogenic on USS | RCC triad → haematuria + flank pain + flank mass (present in <10%) | VHL gene loss → clear cell RCC",t:180},
  {q:"A 70-year-old male has Gleason score 4+4=8, PSA 45 ng/mL, and staging shows no metastases (T2cN0M0). PIRADS 5 lesion on MRI. He is fit for surgery. The most appropriate management is:",a:"Watchful waiting",b:"Radical prostatectomy or radical radiotherapy + androgen deprivation therapy",c:"Active surveillance",d:"ADT alone",ans:"B",e:"High-risk localised prostate cancer (Gleason ≥8, PSA ≥20, or T3-T4) requires radical treatment with curative intent - either radical prostatectomy or external beam radiotherapy combined with long-term androgen deprivation therapy (18-36 months).<br><br>Take Notes: Risk stratification → low (Gleason ≤6, PSA <10, T1-T2a) → active surveillance | Intermediate → prostatectomy or RT ± ADT | High → RT + long-term ADT or prostatectomy | ADT → LHRH agonist (initial testosterone flare → use anti-androgen cover for 4 weeks) or LHRH antagonist (degarelix, no flare) | Metastatic → docetaxel + ADT improves survival",t:180},
  {q:"A 45-year-old female has a 8 mm calculus in the proximal ureter causing hydronephrosis and uncontrolled pain despite analgesia. The most appropriate intervention is:",a:"Increased analgesia and watchful waiting for 4 weeks",b:"Ureteroscopy and laser lithotripsy (URS-LL)",c:"ESWL (extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy)",d:"Open ureterolithotomy",ans:"B",e:"Proximal ureteric stones >7 mm have low spontaneous passage rates and URS with holmium laser lithotripsy is the first-line definitive treatment - it is more effective than ESWL for ureteric stones and avoids repeated ESWL sessions.<br><br>Take Notes: Stone passage rate → <5 mm: 68%, 5-10 mm: 47%, >10 mm: 25% | Tamsulosin → MET (medical expulsive therapy) for stones ≤5 mm (relaxes ureteric smooth muscle) | ESWL → first-line for renal stones <2 cm, poor for proximal ureteric stones | URS → preferred for ureteric stones | PCNL → for large renal stones (>2 cm) or ESWL failure",t:180},
  {q:"Wilms' tumour (nephroblastoma) most commonly presents in children as:",a:"Haematuria with flank mass and hypertension",b:"Asymptomatic abdominal mass discovered incidentally",c:"Painless unilateral abdominal mass in a child aged 3-4 years",d:"Bilateral renal tumours at birth",ans:"C",e:"Wilms' tumour presents as a painless abdominal mass in a child (peak age 3-4 years) - often noticed by parents during bathing. It is smooth, firm, and does not cross the midline (unlike neuroblastoma which crosses midline).<br><br>Take Notes: Wilms' tumour → WT1 gene on 11p13 | Associated syndromes → WAGR (Wilms' + Aniridia + GU abnormalities + Retardation), Denys-Drash, Beckwith-Wiedemann | Treatment → preoperative chemotherapy (SIOP protocol in UK) then nephrectomy | 5-year survival > 85% overall | Bilateral (stage V) → nephron-sparing approaches",t:180},
  {q:"The most appropriate investigation to assess lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS) in a 65-year-old man with BPH is:",a:"Cystoscopy as first-line",b:"Urodynamics immediately",c:"International Prostate Symptom Score (IPSS) + uroflowmetry + post-void residual (PVR)",d:"PSA alone",ans:"C",e:"Initial assessment of LUTS in BPH uses the IPSS questionnaire (symptom severity), uroflowmetry (Q-max <10 mL/s = severe obstruction), and PVR (>150-200 mL suggests significant retention) to guide conservative vs medical vs surgical management.<br><br>Take Notes: Conservative → fluid management, caffeine reduction | Alpha-blockers → tamsulosin (symptom relief, rapid onset) | 5-alpha reductase inhibitors → finasteride (reduces prostate volume, prevents progression) | Combination → superior for moderate-severe LUTS | TURP → gold standard surgical treatment for BPH | Urodynamics → if atypical features or before surgery",t:180},
  {q:"A 25-year-old male presents with sudden onset unilateral testicular pain and swelling. On examination the testis is high-riding with a horizontal lie. The MOST important immediate management is:",a:"Urgent doppler ultrasound to confirm diagnosis",b:"Immediate surgical exploration (bilateral fixation) without delay for imaging",c:"IV antibiotics for epididymo-orchitis",d:"Doppler USS to guide management",ans:"B",e:"Testicular torsion is a clinical diagnosis and surgical emergency - imaging should NEVER delay scrotal exploration when clinical suspicion is high. Orchidectomy is required after 6-8 hours; bilateral orchidopexy is always performed (contralateral fixation prevents future torsion).<br><br>Take Notes: Salvage rates → >4h: 90-100%, 6-12h: 70%, 12-24h: 50%, >24h: <10% | Prehn sign → relief of pain on elevation suggests epididymo-orchitis (NOT reliable) | Torsion of appendix testis → tender blue dot sign at upper pole, less acute | Bell-clapper deformity → high attachment of tunica vaginalis, predisposes to torsion",t:180},
  {q:"The most common malignant germ cell tumour of the testis in young men (20-40 years) is:",a:"Teratoma (non-seminomatous GCT)",b:"Seminoma",c:"Leydig cell tumour",d:"Sertoli cell tumour",ans:"B",e:"Seminoma is the most common testicular germ cell tumour (40-50%), peaks at 30-40 years, and is characterised by exquisite radiosensitivity and chemosensitivity - it has an excellent prognosis even with metastatic disease.<br><br>Take Notes: Seminoma → pure, AFP not elevated, LDH and hCG may be mildly elevated | NSGCT → teratoma, embryonal cell CA, choriocarcinoma, yolk sac → AFP and/or hCG elevated | Treatment → radical orchidectomy through inguinal approach (not scrotal) then surveillance/adjuvant RT or BEP chemotherapy | Cryptorchidism → 4-10x increased testicular cancer risk",t:180},
  {q:"Transitional cell carcinoma (urothelial carcinoma) of the bladder - the most common risk factor is:",a:"Schistosomiasis (squamous cell carcinoma risk)",b:"Cigarette smoking",c:"Phenacetin overuse",d:"Radiation exposure",ans:"B",e:"Cigarette smoking is the most important risk factor for urothelial carcinoma of the bladder, responsible for 50% of cases in men and 35% in women - carcinogenic aromatic amines excreted in urine have prolonged contact with the urothelium.<br><br>Take Notes: Occupational risks → aromatic amines (dye workers, rubber industry, printing) | Schistosomiasis (S. haematobium) → squamous cell carcinoma of bladder (in endemic areas) | Phenacetin + cyclophosphamide → also bladder carcinogens | Haematuria → painless, intermittent = bladder cancer until proven otherwise | NMIBC → TURBT + intravesical BCG/chemotherapy",t:180},
  {q:"Horseshoe kidney is at increased risk of which urological complication?",a:"Renal cell carcinoma",b:"Pelviureteric junction (PUJ) obstruction",c:"Vesicoureteric reflux",d:"Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease",ans:"B",e:"Horseshoe kidney (fusion of lower poles, connected across the midline anterior to the aorta) has a high-riding PUJ with anteriorly placed ureters - abnormal angulation and extrinsic compression by the isthmus predisposes to PUJ obstruction and hydronephrosis.<br><br>Take Notes: Horseshoe kidney → fused lower poles, anterior to aorta, restricted at isthmus (IMA) | Complications → PUJ obstruction, recurrent UTI, renal stones | ESWL → less effective (malrotation causes poor acoustic coupling) | Incidental finding → usually asymptomatic | Wilms' tumour → increased risk in horseshoe kidney",t:180},
  {q:"The classic radiological finding of ureteric colic on CT KUB is:",a:"Bilateral hydronephrosis",b:"Unilateral hydronephrosis + hydroureter proximal to an obstructing calculus with perinephric stranding",c:"Gas in the collecting system",d:"Bladder wall thickening only",ans:"B",e:"CT KUB (unenhanced) for ureteric colic shows the calculus (almost all stones are CT-dense), unilateral hydronephrosis and hydroureter proximal to the obstruction, and perinephric/periureteric fat stranding from ureteric oedema and inflammation.<br><br>Take Notes: CT KUB → sensitivity 94-98%, specificity 96%, no contrast needed | Uric acid stones → radiolucent on plain X-ray but visible on CT | Indinavir stones → CT-negative (not calcified) | Plain AXR → calcium oxalate + calcium phosphate + struvite visible | USS → first-line in pregnancy (avoids radiation)",t:180},
  {q:"The Langer's (tension) lines are important in surgical practice because:",a:"Incisions parallel to Langer's lines heal with minimal scarring",b:"Incisions perpendicular to Langer's lines produce better cosmetic outcomes",c:"Langer's lines define the anatomical planes for flap elevation",d:"They indicate the direction of cutaneous nerve supply",ans:"A",e:"Langer's lines follow the direction of tension in the skin (orientation of collagen fibres in dermis) - incisions parallel to these lines produce less wound tension, better wound healing, and minimal hypertrophic scarring.<br><br>Take Notes: Langer's lines → run parallel to wrinkle lines | Perpendicular incisions → widened, hypertrophic scars | RSTL (relaxed skin tension lines) → clinical equivalent (wrinkle lines) used in planning incisions | Keloid → beyond original wound margins | Hypertrophic → within wound margins",t:180},
  {q:"The 'rule of nines' for calculating TBSA burned in an adult gives which percentage for the anterior trunk?",a:"9%",b:"18%",c:"36%",d:"27%",ans:"B",e:"Wallace's rule of nines: head/neck = 9%, each upper limb = 9%, anterior trunk = 18%, posterior trunk = 18%, each lower limb = 18%, perineum = 1% - totalling 100%. Used for initial fluid resuscitation calculation (Parkland formula).<br><br>Take Notes: Lund and Browder chart → more accurate, accounts for age-related differences in body surface area (especially head-to-leg ratio in children) | Parkland formula → 4 mL/kg/%TBSA over 24h (half in first 8h, half in next 16h) | Only include partial thickness and full thickness burns (not superficial/epidermal)",t:180},
  {q:"A reconstruction ladder in plastic surgery describes which principle?",a:"Use the most complex reconstruction first to ensure best outcomes",b:"Begin with the simplest reconstruction technique and progress to more complex methods only if simpler options are inadequate",c:"All defects should be reconstructed with free flaps for reliability",d:"Local flaps are always superior to skin grafts",ans:"B",e:"The reconstructive ladder (or elevator) principle states that surgeons should use the simplest adequate technique first: primary closure → skin grafts → local flaps → regional flaps → free flaps - complexity escalates only when simpler options are insufficient or unsuitable.<br><br>Take Notes: Reconstructive elevator → allows jumping to most appropriate rung directly when simpler options clearly inadequate | Split-thickness skin graft → contracts more, less durable | Full-thickness graft → less contraction, better colour match | Free flaps → microsurgical, most complex, most reliable blood supply | Perforator flaps → minimise donor site morbidity",t:180},
  {q:"Compartment syndrome of the hand following a crush injury - the compartment containing the highest pressure that requires fasciotomy is:",a:"Thenar compartment",b:"Hypothenar compartment",c:"All compartments including dorsal interossei and carpal tunnel (10 compartments total)",d:"Flexor compartment of the forearm only",ans:"C",e:"The hand has 10 fascial compartments (4 dorsal interosseous, 3 volar interosseous, thenar, hypothenar, adductor pollicis) requiring release by dorsal and volar incisions - forearm fasciotomy may also be required simultaneously.<br><br>Take Notes: Hand compartment syndrome → crush injury, reperfusion, tight cast | Dorsal incisions → releases interosseous compartments | Volar incisions → thenar, hypothenar, adductor | Intrinsic tightness test → measures passive IP flexion with MCP extended vs flexed | Volkmann contracture → untreated forearm compartment syndrome → intrinsic minus deformity",t:180},
  {q:"Dupuytren's disease is characterised by fibroproliferative contracture of:",a:"Flexor tendons of the hand",b:"The palmar fascia (aponeurosis) causing progressive finger flexion contractures",c:"The dorsal extensor apparatus",d:"Carpal tunnel contents",ans:"B",e:"Dupuytren's disease involves progressive fibrosis and contracture of the palmar fascia (not the flexor tendons) - pathological nodules and cords form, causing flexion contractures typically of the ring and little fingers at the MCP and PIP joints.<br><br>Take Notes: Most common in → males, Nordic European ancestry, diabetics, alcoholics, epileptics (phenytoin) | Indication for surgery → MCP contracture >30°, any PIP contracture | Procedures → limited fasciectomy (gold standard), needle aponeurotomy, collagenase (Xiapex) | Diathesis → indicates aggressive disease with high recurrence",t:180},
  {q:"A patient has a 2% deep dermal burn to the right hand from hot liquid. The MOST important aspect of initial management is:",a:"Immediate escharotomy",b:"IV antibiotics prophylactically",c:"Cool running water for 20 minutes, then non-adherent dressing and urgent hand surgery referral",d:"Topical silver sulfadiazine and wound closure",ans:"C",e:"Burns to the hand (a specialist area) require: cool running water for 20 minutes (within 3 hours of injury to reduce tissue damage), non-adherent dressings, and urgent hand surgery referral for assessment and specialised management to preserve function.<br><br>Take Notes: Cooling → 15-20 minutes cool (not ice cold) running water | Hands, face, feet, genitalia, perineum, major joints → all specialist burns areas | Silver sulfadiazine → largely replaced by modern non-adherent dressings (Mepitel, Biobrane) for hand burns | Escharotomy → only for circumferential full-thickness burns (not 2% deep dermal) | Hand burns → physiotherapy from outset to prevent joint stiffness",t:180},
  {q:"A 3-week-old male infant presents with projectile non-bilious vomiting after every feed. He is always hungry. Examination reveals an olive-shaped mass in the epigastrium after a test feed. The diagnosis and treatment are:",a:"Intussusception; air enema reduction",b:"Pyloric atresia; duodenoduodenostomy",c:"Pyloric stenosis; Ramstedt's pyloromyotomy after correction of hypokalaemic hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis",d:"Gastro-oesophageal reflux; conservative management",ans:"C",e:"Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis presents at 3-6 weeks with projectile non-bilious vomiting (hypertrophied pylorus obstructs without involving the bile duct), olive-shaped pyloric mass, and hypokalaemic hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis from HCl loss in vomitus.<br><br>Take Notes: Correct metabolic derangement FIRST before surgery | Diagnosis → USS (pyloric muscle thickness >3 mm, channel length >14-16 mm) | Surgery → Ramstedt's pyloromyotomy (longitudinal incision of pyloric muscle, mucosa intact) | Metabolic correction → IV saline with KCl until alkalosis corrected | Male:female → 4:1, firstborn males most commonly affected",t:180},
  {q:"A 4-month-old infant presents with sudden onset colicky abdominal pain, drawing up legs, with blood and mucus in the stool ('redcurrant jelly') and a right-sided abdominal mass. The diagnosis and initial treatment are:",a:"Necrotising enterocolitis; IV antibiotics and surgical review",b:"Hirschsprung disease; barium enema",c:"Intussusception; air (or hydrostatic) enema reduction",d:"Volvulus; immediate laparotomy",ans:"C",e:"Intussusception (ileo-colic, most common) presents with the classic triad of colicky pain, red-currant jelly stool (ischaemic mucosa), and a right-sided sausage-shaped abdominal mass. Non-surgical air or hydrostatic enema reduction (80-90% success) is first-line if no peritonitis.<br><br>Take Notes: Peak age → 6-18 months | USS → target sign (donut sign) on transverse, pseudokidney sign on longitudinal | Air enema → first-line if no peritonitis/perforation | Surgery → for failed reduction or signs of peritonitis | Pathological lead point (Meckel, lymphoma, polyp) → more common in older children (>2 years)",t:180},
  {q:"A neonate is born with failure to pass meconium in the first 48 hours. Examination reveals abdominal distension. Rectal examination causes explosive passage of gas and stool. Barium enema shows a transition zone at the rectosigmoid junction. The diagnosis is:",a:"Meconium ileus (cystic fibrosis)",b:"Hirschsprung disease (congenital aganglionic megacolon)",c:"Anorectal malformation",d:"Functional constipation",ans:"B",e:"Hirschsprung disease results from failure of ganglion cell migration (neural crest cells) in the bowel wall - aganglionic rectosigmoid causes functional obstruction, failure to pass meconium, explosive decompression on PR, and transition zone on contrast enema.<br><br>Take Notes: Definitive diagnosis → rectal suction biopsy (absence of ganglion cells + hypertrophic nerve trunks + positive acetylcholinesterase staining) | Associated with → Down syndrome, MEN2A, RET mutations | Treatment → pull-through procedure (Swenson, Duhamel, or Soave) | Total colonic aganglionosis → rare, all colon aganglionic | Meconium ileus → inspissated meconium in terminal ileum (CF), Gastrografin enema ± surgery",t:180},
  {q:"Gastroschisis differs from exomphalos (omphalocele) in that gastroschisis:",a:"Has a covering sac of peritoneum and amnion",b:"Is associated with chromosomal anomalies",c:"Is a right-sided defect with no sac, exposed bowel is matted and foreshortened, and associated anomalies are less common",d:"Is located at the umbilicus with intact skin",ans:"C",e:"Gastroschisis is a right-sided (right of umbilicus) abdominal wall defect with no sac - the exposed bowel is characteristically thickened, matted, and foreshortened from prolonged amniotic fluid exposure; chromosomal anomalies are uncommon unlike exomphalos.<br><br>Take Notes: Gastroschisis → no sac, right-sided, low associated anomalies, high intestinal complications | Exomphalos → sac present (peritoneum + amnion), umbilical defect, 50-70% associated anomalies (chromosomal in 30-50%) | Management → wrap in warm saline gauze + plastic bag, IV access, nasogastric decompression, urgent surgical repair | Reduction → primary vs staged (silo) depending on defect size",t:180},
  {q:"A 2-year-old boy has right-sided scrotal swelling that transilluminates and is NOT reducible. The diagnosis and management are:",a:"Inguinal hernia; surgical repair urgently",b:"Hydrocele; surgical ligation of processus vaginalis at 2 years if persistent",c:"Testicular torsion; urgent scrotal exploration",d:"Epididymo-orchitis; IV antibiotics",ans:"B",e:"A non-tender, transilluminable, non-reducible scrotal swelling in a 2-year-old is a communicating hydrocele (patent processus vaginalis) - if it persists beyond 2 years of age without resolution, inguinal exploration with ligation of the processus vaginalis is indicated.<br><br>Take Notes: Hydroceles in infants → 80% resolve spontaneously by 12-18 months via closure of processus vaginalis | Communicating hydrocele → varies in size (larger during day), resolves more slowly | Surgery at 2 years if persistent (inguinal approach, ligation of processus vaginalis) | Tension hydrocele → acute onset, painful → suspect testicular torsion | USS → confirms testicular viability if doubt",t:180},
  {q:"Tracheo-oesophageal fistula (TOF) - the most common anatomical variant is:",a:"H-type fistula without oesophageal atresia",b:"Oesophageal atresia with proximal pouch and distal TEF (Type C, Gross classification)",c:"Oesophageal atresia without fistula (Type A, pure OA)",d:"Double fistula with atresia",ans:"B",e:"Type C (Gross) / Type IIIB (Vogt) - upper pouch atresia with a distal TEF - accounts for 85-90% of all TOF cases. The stomach is gas-filled (air via fistula into stomach) and the upper pouch is gas-filled (blind ending).<br><br>Take Notes: Type A → pure OA (no fistula), gasless abdomen | Type C → most common (85%), gas in stomach | H-type (Type E) → fistula without atresia, presents with recurrent aspiration | Diagnosis → inability to pass NG tube + X-ray showing coiled tube in upper pouch + gas in stomach | Associated anomalies → VACTERL (Vertebral, Anorectal, Cardiac, TEF, Renal, Limb) | Treatment → surgical repair (thoracotomy or thoracoscopic)",t:180},
  {q:"The most common cause of intestinal obstruction in a neonate in the first 24 hours of life is:",a:"Pyloric stenosis",b:"Intussusception",c:"Duodenal atresia (double bubble sign on X-ray)",d:"Meconium ileus",ans:"C",e:"Duodenal atresia presents on day 1 with bilious vomiting and the classic 'double bubble' sign on AXR (dilated stomach + dilated proximal duodenum with no gas beyond the obstruction). Associated with Down syndrome in 30%.<br><br>Take Notes: Double bubble sign → pathognomonic of duodenal atresia | Associated with → Down syndrome (30%), VACTERL, cardiac anomalies | Intrinsic (atresia/stenosis) vs extrinsic (Ladd's bands in malrotation) → both cause proximal duodenal obstruction | Treatment → duodenoduodenostomy (bypass surgery) | Pyloric stenosis → presents at 3-6 weeks, non-bilious",t:180},
  {q:"A 10-year-old girl with known biliary atresia undergoes Kasai hepatoportoenterostomy at 6 weeks of age. She now presents with recurrent bacterial cholangitis. The most appropriate definitive management is:",a:"Repeated courses of IV antibiotics only",b:"Endoscopic biliary stenting",c:"Liver transplantation",d:"Repeat Kasai procedure",ans:"C",e:"Recurrent bacterial cholangitis following Kasai portoenterostomy indicates progressive hepatic fibrosis and end-stage liver disease - liver transplantation is the definitive treatment, and biliary atresia is the most common indication for paediatric liver transplant (accounts for 40-50% of cases).<br><br>Take Notes: Kasai → performed before 60 days of age (best outcomes) | 50% have adequate bile drainage after Kasai | Progressive fibrosis → 70-80% ultimately need transplant by adulthood | Recurrent cholangitis → accelerates fibrosis | Paediatric liver transplant → living-donor left lateral segment most common in children",t:180}
];
